## Foreword by the Governor of the Bank of England

*Signed by **Andrew Bailey**, Governor of the Bank of England*

<!-- page 8: 3 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N001] Personal framing: Bailey did secondary school in the 1970s — a
  terrible decade for economies globally. He did homework by candlelight during
  the **three-day week** and lived through **inflation above 20%** in the UK.
  This early experience made him acutely aware of economics' real-world impact.
  - *Why this matters:* establishes that macroeconomic failure is viscerally felt
    by ordinary people, not just statistics

- [NICE TO KNOW][N002] The book has only **one equation** in its 320 pages — a
  deliberate accessibility choice. This is explicitly stated as unusual for a
  Bank of England publication.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N003] Bank of England's broader economic literacy mission (since 2017):
  - Shorter, plain-language versions of quarterly economic reports
  - **"Knowledge Bank"** section on website covering money, banks, inflation,
    interest rates — attracted more page views than almost any other part of
    the site
  - **Citizens' Panels** across the UK open to any member of the public —
    to hear first-hand economic experiences (cost of living, jobs)
  - **Community Forums** with charities to understand how supported people
    are impacted by the economy
  - 500+ Bank colleagues delivering talks in state schools across the UK
  - Plan to send a copy of this book to every state school in the country

<!-- page 9: 1 nugget -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N004] The Bank's stated mission since 1694: "promote the public good
  and benefit of the people." The foreword wryly notes this sounds better than
  the honest alternative: "help the King fund a war against the French."

<!-- page 10: no content --> *(foreword ends, intro begins)*

