- **Adam Smith** — *The Wealth of Nations*; coined "invisible hand"; quote on trade conspiracies
- **Garrett Hardin** — ecologist/economist, 1960s; coined **Tragedy of the Commons**
- **George Box** — statistician; "All models are wrong, but some are useful"
- **Salvador Dalí** — referenced for "Have no fear of perfection — you'll never reach it"
- **Mark Twain** — "They aren't making it any more" (on land)
- **YKK** — Japanese zip monopoly (real-world imperfect competition example)
- **Microsoft** — 1999 DoJ antitrust case (monopoly stifling innovation)
- **Oxfam** — 2021 study on Pfizer/BioNTech and Moderna vaccine pricing
- **Uber** — 2014 Sydney siege surge-pricing episode

<!-- pp. 59-80 -->

<!-- page 59: overlap — see previous batch -->
<!-- page 60: overlap — see previous batch -->

<!-- page 61: 3 nuggets -->

## Chapter Two (continued): Can Economics Solve Climate Change?

### Network Effects and Natural Monopoly

- [MUST KNOW][N051] **Network effect**: a good or service becomes more valuable
  as more people use it — classic driver of natural monopoly
  - Social media is the paradigm case: a platform nobody else uses
    has near-zero value; one everyone uses has enormous value
  - Facebook ~3 billion users, Twitter ~400 million,
    Instagram ~1.4 billion (as of writing)
  - New entrants face a near-insurmountable barrier: why join
    a network with three users when the dominant one has billions?
  - Result: the market naturally consolidates to one winner —
    not through predatory behaviour but through value dynamics

- [SHOULD KNOW][N052] Network effects extend beyond pure social platforms
  - Amazon Prime + Kindle + Alexa ecosystem: dominance in one
    product sphere creates "networked integration" that generates
    natural monopoly in adjacent spheres
  - Term used: **networked integration**
  - Implication: platform companies are structurally prone to
    monopoly even without explicitly anti-competitive acts

- [SHOULD KNOW][N053] Nuanced view of monopolies: sometimes beneficial,
  sometimes harmful — context determines which
  - High fixed costs → natural monopoly → may benefit consumers
    (railways, energy, finance)
  - Network effects → natural monopoly → may benefit consumers
    (social media, tech platforms)
  - Drug/pharma patents → temporary monopoly → fosters innovation
  - BUT: monopolist can exploit position; hence governments
    regulate natural monopolies (train networks, energy, finance)
  - State + market hybrid: regulated monopoly, not pure competition
  > "All in all, monopolies reveal the perils of placing too much
  > faith in markets left to their own devices."

<!-- page 62: 2 nuggets -->

### UK University Tuition Fees — Externalities Case Study

- [MUST KNOW][N054] **Externality**: the side-effect of an activity on
  people not directly involved in it; causes social cost/benefit
  to diverge from private cost/benefit
  - Classic definition: "the costs or benefits to society as a
    whole are not the same as the costs and benefits to the
    person involved in the activity itself"
  - This divergence is a core mechanism of market failure

- [SHOULD KNOW][N055] UK tuition fee history as externality illustration
  - Pre-1998: zero tuition fees in UK universities
  - Nov 1997: legislation allowed universities to charge max
    £1,000/year (Dearing Report on precarious university finances)
  - 2006: fees rose to £3,000/year in England
  - 2012: tripled to £9,000/year — widespread student protests
  - The debate is fundamentally about the size of positive
    externalities from higher education

<!-- page 63: 3 nuggets -->

### Positive Externalities — Education

- [MUST KNOW][N056] **Positive externality** from higher education:
  private benefit to student < social benefit to society
  - Private benefits: higher lifetime earnings, enjoyment of
    academic environment, social networks
  - Private costs: tuition fees, opportunity cost of not working
  - Social benefits (NOT factored in by individual): more
    productive workforce, more law-abiding citizens, higher
    levels of civic engagement
  - Market failure: individual only buys to the point their
    private need is met; ignores wider social benefit
  - Result: market **under-provides** education relative to
    what is socially optimal

- [SHOULD KNOW][N057] Tuition fee policy maps directly to externality beliefs
  - If positive externalities are small (benefit goes mainly
    to the student) → push for higher student-paid fees
  - If positive externalities are large (whole society gains) →
    push for state subsidy and low/zero fees
  - Most newspaper debates on tuition fees are really debates
    about the size of externalities, even if participants
    don't frame it that way

- [MUST KNOW][N058] **Negative externality** intro — climate change as
  the flipside
  - In 1990, coal electricity cost just over 1p/kWh;
    wind power cost more than 15× that
  - Rational energy company chose coal — not surprising fossil
    fuels made up over 91% of UK energy consumed in 1990
  - The market price of coal electricity did NOT reflect the
    true cost: coal generates ~20× more carbon emissions
    than wind power
  - Carbon emissions → rising global temperatures, erratic
    weather, climate emergency

<!-- page 64: 3 nuggets -->

### Negative Externalities and Climate Change

- [MUST KNOW][N059] The core climate market failure is a negative externality
  - The price of burning fossil fuels doesn't include the
    environmental cost to everyone else
  - Just as positive externalities → under-production,
    negative externalities → **over-production**
  - "Climate change is the ultimate negative externality"
  - Parallel: your heating bill doesn't reflect the true cost
    to the environment — you're being subsidised by the future

- [MUST KNOW][N060] **Tragedy of the Commons** — **Garrett Hardin**'s concept
  applied to climate change
  - Bank of England canteen chip dilemma = microcosm:
    individual interest (maximum chips) undermines collective
    good (enough chips for everyone)
  - Classic applications: overfishing (individual: catch max fish;
    collective: preserve stocks for future generations);
    deforestation (harvest now vs. keep rainforest thriving)
  - Individual interest and collective interest are structurally
    at odds in common resource situations

- [SHOULD KNOW][N061] **Tragedy of the Horizons** — **Mark Carney**
  (former Governor, Bank of England) coined this term
  - Economic evidence shows people heavily discount the future
    relative to the present — short-termism is built-in
  - Climate change, deforestation, long-term infrastructure:
    all operate on multi-generational timeframes that exceed
    most people's planning horizon
  - This short-termism is itself a market failure that compounds
    the negative externality problem

<!-- page 65: 3 nuggets -->

### Economic Solutions to Climate Change

- [MUST KNOW][N062] **Solution 1: Regulation** — government caps on production
  - E.g., limit how much coal can be mined
  - Problem: to set the right quota you must calculate the
    precise size of the externality across hundreds of years —
    "the numbers would be dizzying," any figure is
    "a finger in the wind"
  - Usually we leave such calculations to markets; but here
    the market isn't working — that's the problem
  - Regulatory approach works in theory, is extremely hard
    to calibrate in practice

- [MUST KNOW][N063] **Solution 2: Pigouvian tax** — named for **Arthur Cecil Pigou**
  - Pigou: English economist, wrote famous book on externalities
    in 1920 — long before climate change was an existential concern
  - Idea: tax goods with negative externalities to raise their
    price to reflect true social cost
  - If true cost of fossil fuels (including environmental cost)
    is double the market price → tax should be 100%
  - Effect: decisions remain market-driven, but are now priced
    at their true cost → self-interest aligns with social good
  - For positive externalities: mirror image — a **Pigouvian
    subsidy** lowers the price to the individual to reflect
    the social benefit (free tuition is an example)
  - **The Pigou Club**: group of leading economists advocating
    application of Pigou's ideas to fossil fuels for ~20 years

- [SHOULD KNOW][N064] Calibrating a Pigouvian tax is hard but directionally sound
  - Even imperfect calibration works via law of demand:
    making something more expensive reduces demand for it
  - Elasticities and demand curves still apply even if the
    exact tax rate is uncertain

<!-- page 66: 3 nuggets -->

### Emissions Trading and Elinor Ostrom

- [MUST KNOW][N065] **Solution 3: Cap-and-trade / Emissions Trading Scheme (ETS)**
  — based on **Ronald Coase**'s critique of Pigou (1960s)
  - Coase's argument: externality problems arise not because
    markets fail but because there are **missing markets** —
    no market exists to trade the externality itself
  - Fix: create a market for the right to emit CO₂
  - EU set up the **Emissions Trading Scheme (ETS)** in 2005:
    - EU sets a total cap on CO₂ and other gases
    - Cap reduces over time in line with climate objectives
    - Companies must buy permits to emit; those who reduce
      emissions below their permit can sell the excess
    - Market price of permits reflects the true negative
      externality through supply/demand dynamics
  - At time of writing: 30+ ETS schemes worldwide
    (New Zealand, South Korea, etc.)
  - Key difference from Pigouvian tax: market finds the price
    rather than government setting it

- [MUST KNOW][N066] **Elinor Ostrom** — Nobel Prize-winning economist;
  challenged the inevitability of the Tragedy of the Commons
  - Studied communities worldwide (India, US, Kenya, Turkey)
  - Finding: when communities have control over their own
    common resources, they often do NOT deplete them —
    they come together to ensure sustainability
  - Key condition: those affected by resource depletion must
    have a voice in decision-making
  - Hardin wasn't always wrong; his theory holds in certain
    circumstances, but not universally
  - Policy implication: **community collaboration and
    empowerment** can prevent Tragedy of the Commons without
    state intervention — markets can be saved from themselves

- [SHOULD KNOW][N067] Ostrom vs. Hardin is not a binary
  - Both theories hold under different conditions
  - Hardin: when individuals have no stake in the collective
    outcome and no mechanism for coordination
  - Ostrom: when communities have governance structures and
    control over shared resources
  - Practical upshot: institutional design matters enormously
    for commons management

<!-- page 67: 3 nuggets -->

### Information Asymmetry — The Lemons Problem

- [MUST KNOW][N068] **George Akerlof** — Nobel Prize-winning economist;
  1970 paper "The Market for Lemons"
  - A **lemon** = a dud second-hand car (US slang)
  - Central insight: when buyers can't accurately assess
    quality, they assume average quality and pay average price
  - Consequence: sellers of high-quality cars won't sell
    (can't get a fair price); sellers of lemons will sell
    (getting more than their car is worth)
  - Feedback loop: good cars exit the market → average quality
    falls → buyers lower their quality expectations →
    good cars exit further → "ever-diminishing quality"
  - This is **information asymmetry** as a market failure:
    seller knows far more than buyer about the product

- [SHOULD KNOW][N069] **Adverse selection** — mechanism through which
  information asymmetry distorts market composition
  - Insurance example: insurer can't tell risky from safe driver
  - Default: charge average price; reckless driver gets a deal,
    safe driver overpays
  - Solution attempt: in-car monitoring boxes
  - But adverse selection persists: bad drivers opt out of
    boxes (uncertainty works in their favour); good drivers
    opt in; insurers now treat box-refusal as a risk signal
  - Akerlof's insight arms insurers: "opt-out signals higher risk"

- [SHOULD KNOW][N070] Fake reviews as information asymmetry exploitation
  - 2020: Amazon deleted ~20,000 fake reviews after
    *Financial Times* alleged reviewers were paid for
    5-star reviews
  - Estimate: ~60% of products on Amazon.co.uk had fake reviews
  - Mechanism: sellers exploit buyers' information deficit —
    buyers look for extra signals to compensate for lack of
    direct knowledge; sellers manufacture those signals
  - Direct application of Akerlof's lemons logic to digital markets

<!-- page 68: 2 nuggets -->

### Information Asymmetry and Climate Decisions

- [SHOULD KNOW][N071] Imperfect information compounds the climate crisis
  - People don't know the full environmental cost of their
    choices: is an electric car better if the battery
    production cost exceeds the emissions savings?
  - Without full information, consumers can't make
    socially optimal choices — they may think they're
    reducing negative externalities while actually worsening them
  - "Much like ending up with a dud car when you think
    you've bought a reliable run-around"
  - Information asymmetry is not just a consumer problem —
    it is a structural barrier to solving climate change

### Chapter Two Conclusion — The Bank of England Chip Resolution

- [MUST KNOW][N072] The Bank of England canteen eventually differentiated
  chip prices by portion size
  - Initial uproar; staff had grown accustomed to
    all-you-can-eat pricing
  - Outcome: less food waste; latecomers found chips available;
    people took only what they wanted to eat
  - Lesson: pricing can internalise the cost of overconsumption
    and solve common resource problems

- [MUST KNOW][N073] Chapter Two synthesis — markets fail in multiple ways
  simultaneously; climate change is where they all collide
  - **Under-production** of useful things (education)
  - **Over-production** of harmful things (carbon dioxide)
  - **Monopolies** that act against consumer and social interest
  - **Information asymmetry** that prevents informed decisions
  > "Solving the climate emergency will take a combination of
  > economic understanding, political will and social change.
  > Economic theories, models and studies can only take us so
  > far. But they undoubtedly have a role to play."
  - Markets alone cannot solve climate change; but markets
    WITH the right interventions (Pigouvian taxes, ETS,
    community governance) can be part of the solution

<!-- page 69: no content (OceanofPDF.com watermark page) -->

