<!-- page 71: 3 nuggets -->

## Chapter Three: How Do I Get a Pay Rise?

*On where wages come from, frictions in the Springfield
labour market, and why you should almost always consider
going back to school.*

### Labour Markets — Introduction via Fortnite World Cup

- [SHOULD KNOW][N074] **Fortnite World Cup** (July 2019, Arthur Ashe Stadium,
  New York) as opening case study for labour markets
  - 100 finalists had outperformed 40 million players
  - Most finalists played video games professionally,
    trained thousands of hours for the finals
  - Kyle 'Bugha' Giersdorf (teenager) won $3 million;
    now earns $50,000/month from subscribers watching
    him play online
  - Top gaming celebrities can earn tens of millions/year
    through sponsorship deals
  - Professional e-sports players earn ~$50,000/year as
    base team salary
  - Point: labour markets explain why some people are paid
    enormous sums for video games while others in
    "worthwhile" jobs earn little

- [MUST KNOW][N075] **Marginal product** — the foundational theory of wages
  - Wages at the most basic level should equal how much
    monetary value you add to your employer's output
  - Definition: "how much monetary value your employer makes
    from adding you to their workforce"
  - Higher productivity = higher wage; a baker who makes ten
    cakes/day is worth more than one who makes five
  - Pro gamers are paid millions because they have a high
    marginal product: massive audience demand for their
    entertainment generates revenue far exceeding their salary
  - Authors' Mario Kart video would earn £0 — marginal
    product as YouTube entertainers is essentially zero

<!-- page 72: 3 nuggets -->

### Labour Supply

- [MUST KNOW][N076] **Labour supply** — what determines how many people
  work and how many hours they work
  - Macro level: size of working-age population
    (UK definition: ages 16-64)
  - Labour force changes due to:
    - Net migration (e.g. Miami's working population
      increased 7% in 1980 due to mass Cuban migration
      following Cuban economic downturn)
    - Cultural shifts (women's workforce participation
      has vastly expanded since mid-20th century,
      adding millions to the labour supply)
  - Micro level (personal decision):
    - Whether earnings exceed non-work income
      (welfare payments, investment income)
    - Whether you enjoy the work (non-monetary utility)
    - Whether you prefer leisure to additional income

- [SHOULD KNOW][N077] Labour supply is fluid and non-binary
  - Nobody is permanently in or out of work; supply
    shifts constantly
  - As hourly wages rise, people often work **fewer** hours
    — they need to work less to maintain the same income
  - Example: Spanish TV reporter quit live on air in 2019
    believing she'd won the lottery — illustrating that
    higher income → less work supply
  - People not looking for work (retirees, students,
    caregivers, long-term sick) are NOT counted as
    unemployed — they are outside the labour supply entirely
  - Labour supply "constantly growing and shrinking as a
    result of cultural norms, economic policies and the
    general proclivities of the workforce"

- [SHOULD KNOW][N078] **Derived demand** — the distinctive nature of
  labour demand
  - Labour demand is NOT an end in itself; employers
    don't want workers for their own sake
  - Demand for labour is derived from demand for the
    goods or services workers produce
  - Demand for labour ebbs and flows:
    - Recessions → consumption falls → businesses
      downsize → less demand for workers
    - Automation → robots can substitute for humans →
      reduces demand for certain labour types
  - Key implication: your wage depends partly on how much
    consumers want what you help produce

<!-- page 73: 3 nuggets -->

### Wage Determination by Supply and Demand

- [MUST KNOW][N079] Supply-demand balance determines wages
  - Shortage of workers in a given skill area → wages
    rise (computer scientists, commercial deep-sea divers)
  - Computer scientists: not enough coders relative to
    demand for coding → high wages
  - Commercial deep-sea divers: few people willing to
    spend months on a boat in dangerous conditions →
    compensating premium
  - This is the **invisible hand** trying to recruit people
    into hard-to-fill roles
  - Simplest answer to "how do I get a pay rise?": take
    a job that employers struggle to fill

- [MUST KNOW][N080] **Labour market frictions** — why supply and demand
  don't instantly equilibrate in labour markets
  - Labour markets contain **frictions**: forces that prevent
    supply and demand from matching smoothly and immediately
  - Even with strong demand for labour, wages don't
    always rise to attract more supply instantly
  - Employers and employees often don't even know what
    jobs exist: highly skilled gamers may not realise
    they could be paid to play
  - Result: there will **always** be some unemployment in
    any economy

### History of Unemployment Theory

- [SHOULD KNOW][N081] **Classical economists** (18th-19th century) on unemployment
  - View: unemployment is simply over-supply of labour
  - If more window cleaners than windows to clean →
    wages fall → some workers find wage too low to bother →
    they choose unemployment
  - In this view, unemployment is a **choice**: the market
    wage is below the worker's **reservation wage**
    (the minimum wage at which they'd accept a job)
  - The "classical" view: the market clears if wages
    are flexible — unemployment is voluntary

<!-- page 74: 3 nuggets (continues Ch. 3) -->

<!-- page 75: 2 nuggets (continues Ch. 3) -->

- [MUST KNOW][N082] **John Maynard Keynes** challenged classical unemployment theory
  in the early 20th century
  - Keynes: unemployment is caused by lack of demand for
    goods and services — not worker choice
  - As 20th century progressed, economists increasingly
    recognised labour markets deviate from smooth
    equilibration: not everyone can instantly retrain
    or even know what jobs are available
  - Modern consensus: most unemployment is **involuntary**
    — people are victims of circumstances, not choosers
  - Classical theory → **"new classical" theory**: now focuses
    on changes in labour supply conditions, not just
    over-supply

- [MUST KNOW][N083] **Cyclical unemployment** — unemployment driven by
  economic cycles
  - Economy goes through boom and bust cycles (explored
    in Chapter Nine)
  - During booms: businesses expand, hire → jobs increase
  - During recessions: businesses close, downsize → jobs
    disappear rapidly
  - Labour supply changes much more slowly than labour demand
  - Result: during downturns, many workers are forced into
    involuntary unemployment even though they want to work

- [SHOULD KNOW][N084] The Beveridge Curve — relationship between vacancies
  and unemployment
  - Analysis: compare number of job vacancies to number
    of unemployed
  - Captures the matching problem between workers and jobs
  - (Further detail implied — chapter continues beyond p.80)

<!-- page 76: covered in continuation above -->
<!-- page 77: covered in continuation above -->
<!-- page 78: covered in continuation above -->
<!-- page 79: 3 nuggets -->
<!-- page 80: 2 nuggets -->

<!-- pp. 79-100 -->

## Chapter Three: Why Don't I Earn More?

*On labour markets, wages, bargaining power, and investing in yourself.*

<!-- page 79: 4 nuggets -->

### Compensating Differentials and the Price of Unpleasant Work

- [MUST KNOW][N051] **Compensating differentials**: if a job requires rare skills, long
  training, or is simply very unpleasant, wages rise to compensate — this
  is the invisible hand recruiting people into roles others would avoid
  - Computer scientists are paid a lot because not enough people can code
    relative to demand; commercial deep-sea divers are paid more because
    few want to spend months at sea in dangerous conditions
  - Practical implication: the fastest route to a pay rise is to move
    into a role that is hard to fill
- **Caveat**: "find a hard-to-fill job" is irritatingly useless advice
  for anyone who has struggled with unemployment — it ignores the
  frictions that prevent labour markets from clearing instantly

<!-- page 80: 5 nuggets -->

### Labour Market Frictions and Classical Unemployment Theory

- [MUST KNOW][N052] **Labour market frictions**: forces that stop supply and demand
  matching up smoothly, ensuring there will *always* be some unemployment
  in any economy — markets do not clear instantaneously
- [SHOULD KNOW][N053] **Classical economists' view of unemployment** (18th–19th c.):
  unemployment is a result of over-supply; if there are more workers than
  jobs, wages fall until some workers choose not to work at the going rate
  - Unemployment is *construed as a choice* — workers are deciding the
    wage on offer does not meet their **reservation wage** (the minimum
    at which they consider work worthwhile)
  - Example: surplus of window cleaners → wages fall → some decide not
    to clean windows at the new low wage → they are "choosing" unemployment
- [MUST KNOW][N054] **Keynesian critique** (**John Maynard Keynes**, early 20th c.):
  unemployment is caused by a *lack of demand* for things requiring
  workers — not by workers' own wage preferences; the problem is on the
  demand side of the economy, not the supply side
- [SHOULD KNOW][N055] **"New classical" theory** (modern consensus): economists no
  longer assume people choose unemployment; they increasingly recognise
  workers as victims of **involuntary unemployment** — they focus on
  changes in labour supply and the reasons why markets fail to clear

<!-- page 81: 4 nuggets -->

### Three Types of Unemployment

- [MUST KNOW][N056] **Cyclical unemployment**: unemployment caused by downturns in the
  economic cycle — during recessions businesses close and lay off workers,
  but the labour supply adjusts much more slowly, forcing many into
  unemployment
  - Evidence: **Christopher Dow** and **Leslie Arthur Dicks-Mireaux**
    (1950s) demonstrated cyclical unemployment empirically; they named
    the graphical representation the **Beveridge curve**, after
    **William Beveridge** (founder of the British welfare state), who
    had nothing to do with the curve itself
  - The **Beveridge curve** plots job vacancies against the unemployment
    rate; during a downturn, vacancies fall and unemployment rises
- [MUST KNOW][N057] **Structural unemployment**: unemployment caused by a permanent
  change in what the economy needs — workers have skills that are
  mismatched to available jobs
  - Classic example: US construction workers post-2007–8 financial
    crisis — housing demand collapsed, but the available jobs (e.g.
    bankruptcy lawyers) required entirely different skills; bricklaying
    and cement mixing did not qualify anyone for those roles
  - Structural unemployment takes a long time to fix because it requires
    retraining workers from scratch — "rewiring what jobs people are
    able to do"
  - **Key distinction from cyclical**: an economy can simultaneously
    have high unemployment AND high numbers of unfilled vacancies if
    the mismatch is structural

<!-- page 82: 4 nuggets -->

### Frictional Unemployment and Search-and-Matching Models

- [MUST KNOW][N058] **Frictional unemployment**: unemployment that exists even in a
  healthy, booming economy because of the time lag between leaving one
  job and finding the next — frictions between job-seekers and jobs
  will always prevent zero unemployment
  - In the UK, between 600,000 and 1 million jobs are advertised every
    three months — people often do not know these jobs exist
  - Frictions: drawn-out interview processes, imperfect information
    about what roles are available, the risk of leaving a known job
    for an unknown one
- [SHOULD KNOW][N059] **Search-and-matching models** developed by Nobel Prize winners
  **Peter Diamond**, **Dale Mortensen**, and **Christopher Pissarides**
  — formal models of how long it takes to match people to jobs, taking
  account of information gaps and the complexity of the process
  - These models reduce **matching efficiency** — the economy's
    ability to pair workers with suitable roles
- Technology (LinkedIn, Indeed) has reduced frictions somewhat but
  will never eliminate frictional unemployment; zero unemployment is
  structurally impossible even in a perfect economy
- [NICE TO KNOW][N060] **Policy implication**: governments can reduce cyclical and
  structural unemployment through stimulus and retraining programmes,
  but frictional unemployment is an irreducible baseline — the world
  of work is simply too complicated

<!-- page 83: 5 nuggets -->

### The Simpsons, Bargaining Power, and the Phillips Curve

- [SHOULD KNOW][N061] **Bargaining power framework** (illustrated via *The Simpsons*,
  "Last Exit to Springfield"): employers and workers do not have
  identical bargaining power — the key variable is how easily a worker
  can be *replaced*
  - Mr Burns offers workers a new contract replacing their dental plan
    with a free keg of beer; Homer rallies the union and they strike
  - The ease with which workers can be replaced determines whether they
    can realistically ask for a raise (or dental plan)
- [MUST KNOW][N062] **Phillips Curve**: relationship between unemployment and wages,
  developed by New Zealand economist **William Phillips** in the late
  1950s
  - **Phillips** was an eccentric and visionary figure — in 1949 he
    built an analogue computer that ran on *water* (the **MONIAC** —
    Monetary National Income Analogue Computer) to model how money
    flows through an economy
  - Core relationship: when unemployment is *high*, wage growth is
    *low* (large pool of potential replacement workers weakens
    bargaining power); when unemployment is *low*, wages *increase*
    (employers must bid up to attract or retain workers — "tight"
    labour market)
  - Mechanism: high unemployment → queue of people willing to take
    your job → you can't credibly threaten to leave → employer not
    growing → wage stagnates; low unemployment → employers expanding
    → pool of jobseekers shrinks → must offer more to recruit → wages rise

<!-- page 84: 4 nuggets -->

> "It is particularly important for modelling rates of inflation because
> wage rises are a major contributor to price increases across an
> economy." — *Patel & Meaning on the Phillips Curve's policy role*

- [MUST KNOW][N063] **Phillips Curve — policy importance and controversy**:
  - Used at the heart of economic models for ~50 years; integral to
    central bank policymaking (wage inflation feeds price inflation)
  - **Controversial**: some economists doubt it exists; others think
    it exists some of the time but is unreliable because myriad other
    factors affect the relative power of workers
  - Caveat: economy-wide unemployment data may not reflect local
    conditions — workers are not perfectly mobile, so local monopsonies
    can suppress wages even when national unemployment is low
- [SHOULD KNOW][N064] **Monopsony in labour markets**: when workers in a locality have
  only one realistic employer (e.g. Springfield Nuclear Power Plant is
  by far the largest employer in Springfield), that employer acts like
  a monopoly buyer of labour — it can keep wages and benefits low
  because workers cannot easily leave
  - Workers' relative mobility directly informs their bargaining power

<!-- page 85: 3 nuggets -->

### Automation and Collective Bargaining

- [MUST KNOW][N065] **Automation and worker bargaining power**: technology that
  replaces human tasks reduces workers' relative bargaining power
  against employers — the threat of replacement by machines is real
  - Important caveat: automation is not always bad for workers; some
    robots *complement* existing workers (making them more productive
    and raising wages) rather than replacing them
  - *Simpsons* illustration: Mr Burns brings in robot workers marked
    "100% loyal" — they promptly go rogue ("Crush, kill, destroy") —
    automation can backfire
- [MUST KNOW][N066] **Collective bargaining**: the key driver of labour bargaining
  power is *organisation among workers* — collective power exceeds
  the sum of individual power
  - Homer convinces co-workers to form a union; their collective
    bargaining power exceeds any individual's; Burns eventually accedes
    to their demands
  - Success of strikes depends on **substitutability** of workers —
    how easily they can be replaced; if replacement is hard (as in
    Springfield after the robots malfunction), strikes tend to succeed
  - Wages are ultimately set by marginal product: workers must add
    **marginal product** to be employed; it must be more costly for
    employers to find a replacement than to grant the pay rise

<!-- page 86: 3 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N067] **Strikes and wider economic costs**: increases in labour costs
  can raise the cost of goods/services produced — the cost is passed on
  to consumers
  - In the Springfield example, Burns turning off electricity when the
    strike makes operations unviable illustrates how labour disputes
    can disrupt the entire economy's supply side
- **Core labour market rule**: a worker must add **marginal product**
  in order to be employed; to get a pay rise, they must demonstrate
  higher marginal product — it must be more costly for the employer to
  replace them than to grant the raise

<!-- page 87: 5 nuggets -->

### Human Capital — Investing in Yourself

- [MUST KNOW][N068] **Human capital** (concept developed by American economist
  **Gary Becker**): when you invest in learning new skills, you make
  yourself more productive — and in turn more profitable for your
  employer; more productive workers command higher wages
  - Human capital investment can boost broader economic growth (more
    productive workforce = higher output per worker)
- Ways to build human capital: formal education (school, college,
  university), on-the-job training, apprenticeships, informal learning
  (e.g. gaming, as the *Fortnite* e-sports example illustrates)
- [SHOULD KNOW][N069] **Education and earnings — empirics**:
  - University boosts lifelong earnings by hundreds of thousands of
    pounds; effect is larger for commercially focused subjects
  - In the UK, 5 years after graduation, **medicine, economics, and
    mathematics** graduates earn the most; **art, agriculture, and
    psychology** graduates earn the least
  - Master's degree earns more than a PhD in the UK — PhD holders
    typically want to be university lecturers (lower pay) rather than
    investment bankers; many PhDs are in less commercially focused fields
- [SHOULD KNOW][N070] **Human capital depreciation and hysteresis**:
  - Human capital can *depreciate* — like a professional athlete who
    loses fitness after injury; long-term unemployed workers can lose
    skills, motivation, and training opportunities
  - **Hysteresis**: when this depreciation is *permanent* — skills
    lost in a recession never fully recover; one of the most corrosive
    long-term effects of economic downturns
  - Evidence: students graduating *during* a recession earn less than
    those graduating during a boom, even ten years later — the scar
    persists

<!-- page 88: 4 nuggets -->

### Education as Signal vs. Education as Skill-Building

- [MUST KNOW][N071] **Signalling theory of education** (**Michael Spence**, Nobel
  Prize-winning economist, New York University):
  - Contrarian view: education does not in itself boost your marginal
    product or wage — it *signals* to employers who is high-value
  - In Spence's model, some people are innately more productive than
    others; the employer's problem is distinguishing high-productivity
    from low-productivity workers
  - High-achievers get good grades not because the education trained
    them — they already had high intelligence; the degree signals a
    pre-existing trait, not a created skill
  - **Policy implication**: if you are innately a low-productivity
    worker, spending heavily on education is a bad investment — it
    won't actually raise your marginal product
  - **Consensus caveat**: whether education works via human capital
    (Becker) or signalling (Spence), the outcome is the same for the
    individual — a good grade in a useful subject increases lifetime
    earnings; it is "perhaps the hardest, but most effective, means to
    secure a higher salary"
- [NICE TO KNOW][N072] **Dave Walsh e-sports case study** — microcosm of labour
  market theory:
  - Early 2000s: Walsh quit his post office summer job to play *Halo*
    full-time; his father approved after Walsh proved gaming earnings
    exceeded post office earnings
  - First tournament: won $5,000; two years later signed a $250,000
    three-year contract to join an e-sports team; retired six years later
  - Economic interpretation: Walsh's marginal product at the post
    office was low (franking packages adds little to revenue); his
    marginal product for the e-sports team (prize winnings, sponsorship
    deals) was much higher — he made the economically rational choice

<!-- page 89: 3 nuggets -->

- Chapter conclusion — synthesis of labour market economics:
  - Classical theory: wage set by supply of labour (willingness to
    work) and demand for labour (who will employ you)
  - Modern recognition: frictions mean this never fully clears;
    unemployment never reaches zero; not everyone can immediately
    retrain Dave Walsh-style or even know what better-paid jobs exist
  - Two practical paths to higher earnings: (1) collective bargaining
    if you can organise co-workers; (2) investing in human capital
    — education is the surest long-term route; "think carefully before
    signing up for that PhD"

<!-- page 90: illustration only -->
<!-- page 90: no content -->

