## Chapter Four: Why Am I Richer Than My Great-Great-Grandma?

*On the causes of economic growth, the promise and perils of GDP,
and why the economy is more like a cake than you might think.*

<!-- page 91: 5 nuggets -->

### The Time-Machine Opening — Material Progress Since the 1970s

- [SHOULD KNOW][N073] **Vivid baseline for economic progress**: imagine a time machine
  drops you in Britain in the mid-1970s
  - More than half of households did not own a car; holidays abroad
    were a rare treat
  - In 1977, average household earned less than *half* of 2020 income
    in real terms
  - UK was dubbed "the sick man of Europe"; almost a quarter of the
    population living below the poverty line
- Going further back makes it worse:
  - 1900: average worker earned equivalent of ~£75/week in today's
    money; 1800: ~£25/week
  - The further back you go, the worse off everyone becomes
- Since 1970, average lifespan in Britain has increased by **ten years**
  — material wealth and quality of life are tightly correlated
- [MUST KNOW][N074] **Root cause of inter-generational wealth differences**:
  **economic growth** — the rate at which aggregate income in a country
  increases; growth creates more resources to allocate

<!-- page 92: 4 nuggets -->

### GDP — Origin, Definition, and Formula

- [MUST KNOW][N075] **GDP — origin story**:
  - Great Depression, 1930s: US policymakers knew the economy was
    collapsing but had *no rigorous way to measure it* — impossible to
    design effective policy without measurement
  - US Congress brought in **Simon Kuznets**, economist at the
    **National Bureau of Economic Research (NBER)**
  - Over three years (from 1931), Kuznets' team produced a report
    called *National Income* — this developed **gross domestic product
    (GDP)**, now used by every country in the world to measure economic
    health
  - **Franklin D. Roosevelt's** government used GDP to develop economic
    policies during World War II; other countries then adopted it
- [MUST KNOW][N076] **GDP — three equivalent definitions**:
  1. Total *value* of everything an economy produces that can be bought
     and sold
  2. Sum of everyone's *income* from producing those goods and services
  3. What everyone in the economy has *spent* in a given period
  - All three measure the same thing from different angles — because
    spending = income = value of output in a closed system
- [MUST KNOW][N077] **GDP formula**: GDP = C + I + G + (X − M)
  - **C** = Consumption (households buying goods/services — avocado
    on toast, etc.)
  - **I** = Investment (spending on machinery, equipment, etc.)
  - **G** = Government spending (roads, health service, etc.)
  - **X** = Exports (money flowing in from foreigners buying UK goods)
  - **M** = Imports (money flowing out to buy foreign goods)
  - Net exports (X − M) capture the trade balance contribution
- The book's preferred metaphor: the economy is a **cake** — growth
  makes the cake bigger, meaning larger slices for everyone

<!-- page 93: 5 nuggets -->

### GDP — Flaws, Omissions, and Measurement Problems

- [MUST KNOW][N078] **GDP does not measure quality of life** — Kuznets acknowledged
  this himself:
  - Does not measure happiness, environmental degradation, or inequality
  - Famous quote from **Robert Kennedy**: GDP "measures everything, in
    short, except that which makes life worthwhile"
  - An economy can be growing while its people are getting unhealthier
- [SHOULD KNOW][N079] **GDP omits illegal economic activity** — historically; in 2014
  the UK's **Office for National Statistics** broadened the definition
  to include prostitution and purchases of illegal drugs, causing GDP
  to jump by ~£10 billion (~5%) overnight — not because anything real
  changed, but because the boundary of "the economy" was redrawn
- [MUST KNOW][N080] **GDP omits unpaid household labour** — a major structural flaw:
  - In 2016, unpaid household work (cleaning, cooking, childcare) was
    estimated to add **£1.24 trillion** to the UK economy — equivalent
    to **63% of GDP** at the time — yet counted as zero in GDP
  - Absurd consequence: if you vacuum your own living room, GDP
    is unchanged; if you and your neighbour each pay each other £20
    to vacuum for them, GDP rises by £40 — even though nothing
    economically different has happened
- [SHOULD KNOW][N081] **GDP is notoriously hard to measure**:
  - Economists only survey a *sample* of economic activity and scale it
    up — there is simply too much activity to capture everything
  - In the UK, statisticians collect data from thousands of companies
    (cars made, houses sold, cheese exported) and extrapolate
  - Inevitable error margin — "a very good guess" at best
- Despite all flaws, GDP persists because: (a) it *does* tell you
  something real about quality of life when used carefully; (b) it is
  practical and comparable across countries and time

<!-- page 94: 3 nuggets -->

### GDP Per Capita and the Link to Human Welfare

- [MUST KNOW][N082] **GDP per capita**: GDP divided by population — the more
  meaningful welfare measure than total GDP
  - France and India have similar total GDPs, but France has
    vastly fewer people — per capita, France is more than **twenty
    times richer** per person
  - The *slice of cake* each person gets is what matters for welfare,
    not just the total size of the cake
- [MUST KNOW][N083] **GDP growth reduces poverty** — strong empirical relationship:
  - One study: when GDP per capita *doubles*, income of the bottom
    one-fifth of the population also doubles on average
  - Between 1981 and 2010, China lifted **680 million people** out of
    poverty — more than the entire current population of Latin America
    — largely through economic growth
  - Broadly: higher GDP per capita → lower child mortality, lower
    disease prevalence, better access to education
  - Indonesia example: 1975–2020, GDP per capita grew 3.5% per year
    on average; life expectancy rose from 55 to 72 years; infant
    mortality fell from ~100 to 20 per 1,000 live births
- Growth matters: long-term global trend has been to grow — and some
  growth it has been

<!-- page 95: 4 nuggets -->

### The Scale of Economic Growth and the Solow-Swan Model

- [MUST KNOW][N084] **Scale of global economic growth** (data from *Our World in Data*,
  Oxford University project):
  - 1500: world economy worth ~$431 billion
  - 1700: $643 billion
  - 1900: $3.42 trillion (nearly 8x the 1700 figure)
  - 2015: ~$108 trillion — **250 times** bigger than in 1500
  - Cake metaphor: if the 1500 economy fit in a 20x10 cm tin, the
    2015 economy is bigger than a table tennis table — and each
    person's slice is more than fifteen times bigger
  - "We're not just richer than our ancestors: we're almost
    inconceivably richer."
- [MUST KNOW][N085] **Solow-Swan model** — the dominant framework for understanding
  where growth comes from, developed by economists **Robert Solow**
  and **Trevor Swan**, building on **Adam Smith** and **David Ricardo**:
  - Four **factors of production** drive economic growth:
    1. **Land** — the space on which you build your economy
    2. **Labour** — the people producing goods and services
    3. **Capital** — the machines that help workers produce more
    4. **Technology** — the efficiency with which labour and capital
       combine (also called **total factor productivity**)
  - Caveat: these four factors can be overstated — sociological,
    historical, and political reasons also explain why some economies
    are bigger than others

<!-- page 96: continuation — next batch will pick up -->

