### Named Individuals, Thinkers, and Works Referenced (pp. 79–100)

- **John Maynard Keynes** — demand-side theory of unemployment
- **Christopher Dow** and **Leslie Arthur Dicks-Mireaux** — Beveridge
  curve empiricists (1950s)
- **William Beveridge** — founder of British welfare state (curve
  named after him)
- **Peter Diamond**, **Dale Mortensen**, **Christopher Pissarides** —
  Nobel Prize winners; search-and-matching models of frictional
  unemployment
- **William Phillips** — New Zealand economist; Phillips Curve;
  MONIAC water computer (1949)
- **Gary Becker** — American economist; human capital theory
- **Michael Spence** — Nobel Prize winner, NYU; signalling theory of
  education
- **Dave Walsh** — e-sports player; *Halo*; illustrates marginal
  product theory
- **Simon Kuznets** — economist, NBER; invented GDP; *National Income*
  report (1931)
- **Franklin D. Roosevelt** — used GDP for wartime economic policy
- **Robert Kennedy** — famous critique of GDP ("measures everything
  except that which makes life worthwhile")
- **Robert Solow** and **Trevor Swan** — Solow-Swan growth model;
  factors of production
- **Adam Smith**, **David Ricardo** — cited as foundational influences
  on growth theory
- *Our World in Data* (Oxford University project) — global GDP
  historical data source
- *The Simpsons* — "Last Exit to Springfield" episode used to
  illustrate bargaining power, monopsony, automation, and collective
  bargaining throughout Chapter Three

<!-- pp. 99-120 -->

## Chapter Four (continued): Why Are You Richer Than Your Great-Great-Grandparents?
*On GDP, economic growth, and its discontents*

<!-- page 99: 4 nuggets -->

### GDP Per Capita — the More Honest Measure

- **GDP at face value** misleads on living standards: France and India
  have similar total GDP, but France is far richer *per person*
  — France's population is ~20x smaller, so each person's share
  is more than twenty times bigger [MUST KNOW][N051]
- **GDP per capita** = GDP divided by total population; the standard
  measure economists use to compare living standards across countries
- Rule of thumb (with exceptions): when GDP per capita doubles,
  the income of the bottom fifth of the population also doubles
  on average [SHOULD KNOW][N052]
  - Evidence: **China lifted 680 million people out of poverty**
    between 1981 and 2010 — more than the entire current population
    of Latin America — largely through economic growth
- GDP growth correlates with better health, especially in developing
  countries: child mortality falls, disease prevalence drops
  - **Indonesia example**: 1975–2020, GDP per capita rose 3.5% per
    year on average; life expectancy rose from 55 to 72 years;
    infant mortality fell from ~100 to 20 per 1,000 live births
    [SHOULD KNOW][N053]
- GDP growth funds better education — higher-income countries see
  greater primary/secondary school attendance, leading to
  higher-paying jobs in the next generation

<!-- page 100: 3 nuggets -->

### The Long-Run Growth of the World Economy

- **Our World in Data** (Oxford University project) data on world GDP:
  - 1500: $431 billion
  - 1700: $643 billion
  - 1900: $3.42 trillion (~8x the 1700 figure)
  - 2015: ~$108 trillion — 250x the 1500 figure [MUST KNOW][N054]
- Per-person slice in 2015 was more than **15 times bigger** than
  in 1500 — "we're not just richer than our ancestors: we're almost
  inconceivably richer"
- The cake metaphor: if 1500 world GDP fit a 20x10 cm tin, 2015
  GDP would be bigger than a table tennis table

> "We're not just richer than our ancestors: we're almost inconceivably richer. But why?"

### The Four Factors of Production (Solow-Swan Framework)

- Most famous growth model comes from economists **Robert Solow**
  and **Trevor Swan**, building on **Adam Smith** and **David Ricardo**
- They identified **four factors of production** — the ingredients
  of economic growth: [MUST KNOW][N055]
  1. **Land** — the physical space (and natural resources) on which
     the economy is built
  2. **Labour** — the people who produce goods and services
  3. **Capital** — the machines and physical assets that help workers
  4. **Technology** — the efficiency with which labour and capital
     combine (the "multiplier")
- Caveat: sociological, historical, and political factors also matter;
  these four are a useful framework, not the complete picture

<!-- page 101: 3 nuggets -->

### Factor 1: Land

- Land = physical space for buildings + **natural resources** (iron
  ore, coal, water)
- Land is the **hardest factor to acquire** — once used up, hard to
  get more; it is a fixed asset
  - Example: UAE built artificial Palm Islands off Dubai (2001)
    to expand land supply
- Natural resources matter for production: Taiwan's 2021 drought
  (worst in 50 years) strained semiconductor production, which
  requires large water volumes
- Countries that must *import* resources face reduced margins,
  lowering achievable GDP

### Factor 2: Labour

- Larger populations = more potential workers = more output
- Two largest economies (USA: 329M, China: 1.4B) are also the
  two most populous nations — not coincidence [SHOULD KNOW][N056]
- But population *size* isn't everything — **demographics matter**:
  - Higher-educated labour pools produce higher-value outputs,
    growing the economy faster

<!-- page 102: 3 nuggets -->

### Labour: Demographics and Barriers

- **Ageing populations drag on growth**: older people are less likely
  to be economically active [MUST KNOW][N057]
  - **Japan** example: ~30% of population is 65+; over 86,000
    centenarians; some economists estimate Japan's GDP will decline
    by 25%+ over next 40 years due to demographics alone
  - US data: a 10% increase in the share of over-60s reduces GDP
    per capita growth by 5.5%; US population aged 65+ is set to
    rise ~70% between 2020 and 2060

- **Gender barriers to employment** reduce labour supply significantly:
  - US: ~57% of women are employed
  - Algeria: only ~17% of women are employed
  - If US women's workforce participation (1970–2016) had matched
    Norway's (76% of working-age women employed), the US economy
    would be ~$1.6 trillion larger — ~$5,000 more per person
    [SHOULD KNOW][N058]

<!-- page 103: 3 nuggets -->

### Factor 3: Capital

- **Capital** = physical assets (machinery, factories, computers)
  — the "wooden spoon" in the cake analogy; it helps workers
  produce more or faster [MUST KNOW][N059]
  - Without scissors and sewing machines, clothes factory workers
    can't make clothes; without computers, accountants can't work
- Capital **depreciates**: equipment breaks, software goes obsolete
  — without continuous capital investment, growth stalls
- Key insight: capital is not an ingredient itself but a tool;
  it is consumed over time and must be replaced/upgraded

### Factor 4: Technology — the Most Important Ingredient

- Solow & Swan's great insight: **technology is the multiplier**
  that determines how efficiently the other three factors combine
  [MUST KNOW][N060]
- Technology = anything that improves the use of other factors;
  it is "know-how" — not just gadgets but also rule of law,
  the wheel, patents, better processes
- Technology enables more output from the *same* inputs: same land,
  same labour, same capital → more goods produced

<!-- page 104: 3 nuggets -->

- Unlike land/labour/capital, technology is **non-rival**: one person
  using knowledge doesn't prevent another from using it
- **Ha-Joon Chang** (University of Cambridge economist) argues the
  **washing machine** has done more to drive growth than the internet:
  it freed people (especially women) from domestic chores,
  effectively **doubling the labour supply** [SHOULD KNOW][N061]

> "Technology might be the most important ingredient in economic
> growth."

- Conclusion on growth: you are richer than your ancestors because
  of accumulated improvements in technology, which raised the
  efficiency of land, labour, and capital over centuries

<!-- page 105: 3 nuggets -->

### Growth's Dark Side: The Three "Bumps"

- Politicians and economists are obsessed with growth — when it
  shrinks, it's seen as a national disaster (e.g., 2007–8 crisis)
- But growth is NOT an unambiguous good; three key problems:
  1. **Inequality** — growth can widen income gaps
  2. **Happiness** — beyond a threshold, growth stops improving
     wellbeing
  3. **Environmental destruction** — growth consumes natural
     resources and may be unsustainable
- **Simon Kuznets** (the man who pioneered GDP measurement) raised
  the inequality problem himself in a 1955 paper [SHOULD KNOW][N062]

### Bump 1: Inequality — The Kuznets Curve

- Kuznets' argument: in early stages of growth, income inequality
  *rises*; as economies mature, inequality eventually *falls*
  — an inverted-U curve later named the **Kuznets Curve**
- Mechanism: technological change disrupts old jobs; benefits flow
  unevenly; some workers' skills become obsolete [MUST KNOW][N063]
- Example: **supermarket self-checkouts**
  - Benefits customers (faster) and owners (cheaper than cashiers)
  - Cashiers lose jobs; skilled managers keep theirs and may get
    pay rises from increased revenues
  - Economy grows MORE PRODUCTIVE → but inequality grows too

<!-- page 106: 3 nuggets -->

### How the Kuznets Curve Works in Practice

- Agricultural-to-industrial transition: factory owners in cities
  can hire cheap rural labour → urban incomes rise, rural incomes
  stagnate or fall; income gap widens [SHOULD KNOW][N064]
- Kuznets believed the inequality would eventually self-correct:
  - Factory owners raise wages as they get richer
  - Low-paid workers invest in education/skills and move up
  - Eventually the cashier finds another (possibly better) job
    as growth spreads through the economy
- Caveat: many modern economists dispute whether the Kuznets Curve
  self-corrects in today's technology-driven economies

### Bump 2: Happiness — The Easterlin Paradox

- **Richard Easterlin** (University of Pennsylvania economics
  professor) studied happiness data from the 1970s onward
  [MUST KNOW][N065]
- First finding: richer people ARE happier than poorer people;
  richer countries ARE happier than poorer countries
  - Within a country: only 25% of the lowest income group
    reported being "very happy" vs. ~50% in the highest group
- BUT: **Easterlin Paradox** — once a nation's wealth reaches
  a certain level, happiness *levels off* or even *declines*
  over time [MUST KNOW][N066]
  - In Chile, China, and South Korea: GDP per capita doubled
    but overall happiness *fell slightly*
  - Individual-level threshold: happiness rises up to ~$75,000
    salary ($90,000 in today's money); beyond that, further
    income gains do NOT increase happiness

<!-- page 107: 3 nuggets -->

### Why the Easterlin Paradox Happens

- **Easterlin's explanation — social comparison**: people with
  higher incomes are happy partly because they are richer
  *relative to others*; when everyone gets richer, the gap
  narrows and the relative benefit disappears [SHOULD KNOW][N067]
- **Diminishing marginal utility of income**: each extra pound
  earned delivers less happiness than the previous one
  — same principle seen in business (diminishing marginal returns)
  - Example: buying a first car dramatically improves your life;
    buying a fourth car barely moves the needle

### Bump 3: The Environment

- Economic growth consumes **natural resources** (oil, cobalt,
  water, land) — in the short term this improves quality of life,
  but depleting the natural world reduces quality of life long-term
  [MUST KNOW][N068]
- Growth's relationship with environmental damage — not new:
  the **Easter Island** parable:
  - Pre-15th century: population of 10,000–20,000 on a thriving
    island economy
  - Islanders chopped down forests (for moai-transport tools
    or fishing canoes)
  - Without trees: soil erosion → crop failures → civil war
    → population collapsed to ~3,000 by 1720s; island almost
    entirely deforested by 1722
  - Lesson: overexploitation of natural resources drove civilisation
    collapse; used as parable for global growth risks [SHOULD KNOW][N069]

<!-- page 108: 2 nuggets -->

- Nuance: some anthropologists say Easter Island's collapse was
  more complex (disease from European contact also a factor);
  but economists still use it as a parable

### The Hidden Cost: Natural Capital

- Most economic models **omit the environment** entirely — they
  don't account for depreciation of natural resources
  [MUST KNOW][N070]
- Why omitted: hard to put a monetary value on nature
- Studies estimate 2011 value of the **entire biosphere** was
  $125–145 trillion/year — vs. global GDP of $72 trillion at
  the time; nature is worth more than the entire formal economy
- Term: **natural capital** — the economic value of clean air,
  clean water, green space, biodiversity

<!-- page 109: 3 nuggets -->

### Climate Change as the Ultimate Growth Risk

- Climate change may reduce global GDP by **nearly 15% by 2050**
  according to some studies [MUST KNOW][N071]
- 2018 California wildfires cost the US economy ~£110 billion
  (~0.7% of US GDP in that year) — a single climate event
- Growth now could **impede growth later**: models that ignore
  environmental costs are "particularly myopic"

### Chapter Conclusion

- GDP growth has overwhelmingly improved human welfare historically
  — better healthcare, education, food, consumer goods
- But it is not unconditionally good: it can bring inequality,
  stagnating happiness, and environmental destruction
- Technology is the key to **green growth**: many economies
  investing in green technology can tackle environmental damage
  while continuing to grow GDP
- The tension between growth and sustainability is not resolved —
  "economics alone cannot offer all the solutions"

<!-- page 110: 1 nugget -->
*(Page 110 is the chapter-divider illustration — a drawing of a T-shirt.
No text content.)*

