## Chapter Five: Why Are So Many of My Clothes Made in Asia?
*On comparative advantage, the remarkable power of trade,
and the great bra shortage of 2005*

<!-- page 111: 3 nuggets -->

### The Global Supply Chain

- Everything around us is made in multiple countries:
  an IKEA Billy bookshelf sold in the UK is designed in Sweden,
  uses wood from Poland, screws from China and Poland,
  manufactured in Germany, distributed globally [SHOULD KNOW][N072]
- **Smartphones** travel around the world ~20 times during
  manufacture, drawing on inputs from ~50 countries
- Aircraft wings cross borders ~12 times before being attached
  to a plane
- This is not new — trade has happened for centuries; the principle
  predates planes and the internet

### The Triathlete Analogy: Power of Specialisation

- **Jennifer Clark** (Bank of England): completed the 2021 Europe
  Triathlon Championships in 2h 11m — fast by amateur standards
- World record holder (standard-distance triathlon): **Simon Lessing**
  (British) — completed the 51.5km Olympic course in 1h 39m 50s
  at the 1996 Triathlon World Championships
- BUT specialist world records beat the triathlete:
  - Swimming (1.5km): **Sun Yang** (China) — 14m 31s
  - Running (10km): **Joshua Cheptegei** (Uganda) — 26m 11s
  - Cycling (40km): **Victor Campenaerts** (Belgium) — ~43m

<!-- page 112: 3 nuggets -->

- Even the world's best triathlete is *slower* than specialist
  world record holders in each individual discipline
- **Key economic principle**: the power of **specialisation**
  — focusing on one narrow skill makes you better at it, and
  frees others to be better at what they do [MUST KNOW][N073]
- **Adam Smith** in *The Wealth of Nations*: specialisation by
  workers is the primary force driving economic efficiency;
  he called this the **division of labour**

### Adam Smith's Pin Factory

- Smith visited a pin factory and observed: each pin requires
  ~18 distinct tasks (drawing wire, cutting, attaching head, etc.)
  [MUST KNOW][N074]
- If 10 workers each did all 18 tasks: ~2 pins per worker per day
  = ~20 pins total
- If each worker **specialised in one task**: same 10 workers
  could produce **48,000+ pins per day**

> "In this manner, divided into about eighteen distinct operations,
> which, in some manufactories, are all performed by distinct hands."
> — Adam Smith, *The Wealth of Nations*

<!-- page 113: 3 nuggets -->

### Why Specialisation Works (Smith's Three Reasons)

1. **No time lost switching tasks** — not just physically moving
   between stations but mentally getting into each new role
2. **Lower training costs** — teach each worker one task instead
   of all eighteen; saves time and money, boosts profit margins
   (and potentially wages) [SHOULD KNOW][N075]
3. **Expertise compounds** — repetition builds skill; workers
   naturally gravitate toward the task they're best at (better
   eyesight → fiddly work; better strength → heavy work) →
   better quality output overall

- Impact: Smith's pin factory insight was so important that the
  *old* £20 Bank of England banknote depicted the pin factory
  on its reverse, with the quote about division of labour

### Triathlete Relay — Quantifying Specialisation Gains

- If the world's best specialist swimmer, cyclist, and runner
  each did their leg of the triathlon relay:
  - They would beat Simon Lessing's world record by
    **15 minutes and 12 seconds**
  - Triathletes lose 30–45 seconds *just changing kit* between
    legs; three specialists eliminate this loss

<!-- page 114: 2 nuggets -->

### From Pin Factory to Global Economy

- Modern factories have taken specialisation far beyond Smith's
  vision: many make **only one component** of a final product
  — one microchip type, one car part
- Individual components have little value alone, but combine
  with other specialist parts to make goods everyone depends on
- The principle scales from workers → factories → **countries**

### Countries as Specialists: Absolute Advantage

- Countries specialise in what they're best at and **trade** the
  surplus: Taiwan (microchips), Germany (cars), France (wine),
  UK (financial services) [MUST KNOW][N076]
- **Absolute advantage**: one country can produce a good more
  cheaply or efficiently than any other, often due to climate,
  history, or accumulated expertise
  - Britain has no banana farms: Ecuador has an absolute advantage
    in bananas; British climate is simply worse at growing them
- Specialisations self-reinforce: as more people train in a sector,
  the country gets even better (UK financial services example —
  legal system + educated workforce attracted people, who built
  more expertise)
- Geography-driven specialisation: **Cremona, Italy** — 16th
  century violin-making hub because local Lombardy region had
  maple and spruce (the two essential violin woods); sector
  accumulated momentum; today Cremona produces 80% of Italy's
  exported bowed instruments [NICE TO KNOW][N077]

<!-- page 115: 3 nuggets -->

### Beyond Absolute Advantage: Comparative Advantage

- Problem: what if one country is better at *everything*?
  Does the other country have nothing to offer?
- Thought experiment: UK climate improves due to global warming →
  southern England becomes tropical → could UK grow bananas?
- Answer: probably still worth importing from Ecuador
  — because UK land is *scarce* and already used to grow
  strawberries, potatoes, wheat [MUST KNOW][N078]

### The Mechanics of Comparative Advantage

- **Comparative advantage** = produce the thing with the *lowest
  opportunity cost* relative to other countries [MUST KNOW][N079]
- Example (UK vs Ecuador — strawberries and bananas):
  - UK: growing bananas costs £1; strawberries cost 50p
    (bananas cost 2x strawberries)
  - Ecuador: growing bananas costs 10p; strawberries cost 40p
    (strawberries cost 4x bananas)
  - Ecuador has **absolute advantage** in both fruits
  - BUT: Ecuador's opportunity cost of strawberries is very high
    (giving up 4 bunches of bananas per punnet of strawberries)
  - UK's opportunity cost of bananas is lower in relative terms
    for strawberries
  - Optimal: Ecuador focuses on bananas; UK focuses on strawberries;
    both trade → **both countries are better off** [MUST KNOW][N080]

<!-- page 116: 2 nuggets -->

- This result is **counterintuitive**: even if Ecuador can grow
  both fruits more cheaply, it still benefits from specialising
  in just bananas and trading for strawberries
- **Comparative advantage** was one of the great theoretical
  innovations of 19th-century economics — introduced by
  **David Ricardo** (referenced, to be met in next section)

### National Specialisation in Practice

- Countries do not choose specialisations arbitrarily:
  they emerge from climate, legal institutions, history, luck,
  and accumulated expertise
- UK in financial services: strong legal system + educated
  workforce → attracted financial firms → more people trained →
  self-reinforcing specialisation advantage [SHOULD KNOW][N081]

<!-- page 117–120: notes cover continuation of Ch. 5
 — pages 117-120 were NOT visible in this batch;
 the batch ended mid-Chapter 5 on p.116 -->

