## Chapter 5 (continued): Why are your T-shirts made in Asia?

### Specialisation From Local Geography

- **Cremona, Italy** — violin-making capital since 16th century;
  started because royal families demanded instruments and two
  essential violin woods (maple + spruce) grew in the Lombardy
  region; craftsmen accumulated expertise → self-reinforcing
  specialisation; today Cremona produces **80% of bow-stringed
  instruments exported from Italy** [SHOULD KNOW][N082]
- Lesson: specialisations often begin with a geographic accident,
  then compound through human capital accumulation over generations
- Distinction between **absolute advantage** (better at something
  in absolute terms — Ecuador grows bananas cheaper than UK in all
  scenarios) vs **comparative advantage** (what you give up
  matters — Ecuador's opportunity cost of strawberries is very
  high, so it should still focus on bananas) [MUST KNOW][N083]

<!-- page 119: 2 nuggets -->

### Comparative Advantage When Climate Changes

- Thought experiment: what if climate change makes southern England
  tropical? Would the UK now grow bananas instead of strawberries?
  Answer: probably still **No** — because UK land is scarce
  (especially southern coast), switching to bananas means giving up
  strawberries, potatoes, wheat
- This illustrates the key insight: comparative advantage is about
  **opportunity cost**, not just absolute capability [MUST KNOW][N084]
- Even if you *can* produce something cheaply, you should only do
  so if the opportunity cost is low relative to other countries

<!-- page 120: 2 nuggets -->

### David Ricardo and the Theory of Comparative Advantage

- **David Ricardo** — the great economist who formalised
  comparative advantage; born ~50 years after **Adam Smith**;
  wealthy businessman who came to economics by reading
  *The Wealth of Nations*; applied Smith's factory-level
  specialisation to *entire nations* [SHOULD KNOW][N085]
- Ricardo's argument: an economy boosts growth by focusing on the
  industry where it has greatest *comparative* advantage
- Critical addition: comparative advantage only delivers its full
  benefits **if trade exists** — countries must exchange with
  nations that have complementary advantages; without trade,
  each country is stuck consuming only what it produces best

<!-- page 121: 2 nuggets -->

### History of Global Trade

- Trade is ancient: the **Silk Road** linked China and the Roman
  Empire from the 2nd century BCE onwards [NICE TO KNOW][N086]
- By the 17th century, Europeans could buy potatoes, tomatoes,
  coffee, and chocolate — all American products — via trade
- **19th-century steamships and railways** dramatically expanded
  trade volumes
- **Keynes (1919)** captured the pre-WWI globalised world:

> 'The inhabitant of London could order by telephone, sipping
> his morning tea in bed, the various products of the whole
> Earth, in such quantity as he might see fit, and reasonably
> expect their early delivery upon his doorstep.'
> — *John Maynard Keynes*, 1919

<!-- page 122: 2 nuggets -->

### The Modern Scale of Globalisation

- Global trade has increased by more than **4,000% since 1900**
  [MUST KNOW][N087]
- **Globalisation** = growing interdependence of world economies;
  the vast majority of goods consumed anywhere were sourced from
  multiple countries
- Why T-shirts are made in Asia: China's comparative advantage is
  **low labour costs** — average Chinese worker earned ~$14,000
  (90,501 yuan) in 2019 vs $53,490 average US salary [MUST KNOW][N088]
  - Fewer, more-educated US workers → higher wages → unskilled
    labour-intensive tasks like T-shirt making shift to developing
    economies
  - China: 128 million manufacturing jobs, nearly **one-third of
    China's GDP**

<!-- page 123: 2 nuggets -->

### Modern Trade: Goods, Services, and Digital

- Physical goods still dominate: **77% of global trade** in 2020
  (World Trade Organization / **WTO**) [SHOULD KNOW][N089]
- But supply chains are now hyper-specialised:
  - In 2020 **Apple** sourced parts from nearly **30 countries**
    for iPhones — each country making a highly specialised component
- Services trade = **23% of global trade**; based on exchange of
  ideas (R&D, legal advice, finance) more than labour; relies on
  technology/internet rather than physical shipping

<!-- page 124: 2 nuggets -->

### The Hidden Scale of Digital Trade

- **Digital trade** (email, YouTube, streaming, social media) is
  largely NOT included in official trade statistics
- If counted, digital flows would add **$8.3 trillion** in value
  annually — an increase of ~**20% of total trade flows**
  [SHOULD KNOW][N090]
- Shift from CDs/DVDs to streaming is a form of trade in
  information and data rather than physical goods

<!-- page 125: 1 nugget -->

### The Dark Side of Free Trade: Offshoring and Political Backlash

- Is more trade always good? Many argue not — especially for
  workers in manufacturing sectors of rich countries [MUST KNOW][N091]
- Since late 20th century, rich countries saw manufacturing sectors
  decline as jobs were '**offshored**' to lower-income countries
  with cheaper labour
  - In mid-20th century, T-shirts were probably made in Britain;
    now they are not
- Political backlash against free trade has grown across the
  developed world over the past 20+ years
- Some countries responded with **protectionism** — policies to
  shield domestic industries from foreign competition

<!-- page 126: 2 nuggets -->

### Case Study: The Bra War of 2005

- Background: the **Multi Fibre Agreement** (later *Agreement on
  Textiles and Clothing*) imposed worldwide **quotas** on textiles
  — limiting how many clothes developing countries like China
  could export to developed countries like EU members
  - Purpose: protect EU/US clothing manufacturers from cheap
    competition [SHOULD KNOW][N092]
- Early 2005: the Agreement was abolished → quota-free trade in
  clothes began
- Result: US saw **100% increase** in clothing imports from China;
  EU saw **200%+ increase** — within months
- Spain, Italy, and other EU countries with long-established
  domestic clothing manufacturing were suddenly exposed to
  unprecedented competition

<!-- page 127: 2 nuggets -->

### The Bra War Escalates

- EU response: imposed **annual quotas** limiting some Chinese
  clothing imports
- China retaliated: **'dumped'** (flooded) more than a quota's
  worth of clothes into Europe within a few months
- EU then refused to allow many of those clothes into shops →
  result: **80 million clothing items stranded in ports**,
  including [MUST KNOW][N093]:
  - 4 million bras (hence "the **Bra War**")
  - 48 million jumpers
  - 17 million pairs of trousers
- Who won? No one:
  - Consumers faced shortages and had to buy from
    less-efficient producers at higher prices
  - European retailers couldn't source at prior prices
  - Chinese manufacturers lost access to European market
  - EU and China eventually compromised: excess allowed to count
    against following year's quota

<!-- page 128: 2 nuggets -->

### Trade Wars: No Winners

- Lesson from Bra War generalised: **trade wars produce no winners
  in the long run** [MUST KNOW][N094]
- US–China trade war (beginning 2018): expected to lower both
  countries' GDP by ~**2%** and global GDP by ~**1%** at the time
- EU's longer-term solution: shift focus toward **high-value-added
  production** (wind turbines, electric vehicles) where they have
  comparative advantage — moving away from labour-intensive
  clothing manufacturing
- Risk of trade wars can never be fully eliminated as long as
  states have competing interests

<!-- page 129: 2 nuggets -->

### Trade Blocs as a Solution to Trade Wars

- Countries reduce trade war risk via **trade agreements**:
  bilateral (two states) or **trade blocs** (many countries
  commit to shared trading rules) [SHOULD KNOW][N095]
- Blocs typically: reduce or eliminate tariffs among members,
  negotiate shared product standards
- Trade wars unlikely between bloc members with common protocol
- Oldest significant bloc: **Zollverein** (German customs union),
  1834 — covered most of the German Confederation (now Germany,
  Austria, Czech Republic, Luxembourg, etc.)
  - Before Zollverein: ~**1,800 customs barriers** within the
    Confederation; eliminating them boosted trade and profits
- Modern examples:
  - **European Union** — effectively zero cross-border shipping
    costs within member states
  - **NAFTA** (now **USMCA** — United States-Mexico-Canada
    Agreement)
  - **RCEP** (Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership, 2020):
    largest ever trade agreement — brought together **ASEAN** plus
    China, Japan, South Korea, Australia, New Zealand; those
    countries represented ~**30% of world GDP** in 2019

<!-- page 130: 2 nuggets -->

<!-- page 131: 1 nugget (RCEP and trade blocs context continues) -->

### Who Exports What — Clothes vs Services

- In 2020, China exported just under **one-third of the world's
  clothes**; Bangladesh and Vietnam ~6% each; then Turkey and
  India [SHOULD KNOW][N096]
- But shift to services trade changes the picture entirely:
  - **US exports 14% of world's services** — far ahead of all
    other countries
  - UK and Germany next; **China (the world's largest clothes
    exporter) is 5th in services, behind France**
  - China's comparative advantage is in manufacturing, not
    knowledge-intensive services

<!-- page 132: 1 nugget -->

### Comparative Advantage Is Dynamic, Not Fixed

- One of the great rules of economic history: **comparative
  advantages change over time** [MUST KNOW][N097]
- Asia was once purely cheap-labour clothes exporter; it has since
  become world's primary producer of mobile phones, computers,
  and solar panels; may become leading car exporter within decades
- Implication: today's largest producers may one day be importing
  the very goods they currently dominate
- Comparative advantage sources:
  - Resource-based (Switzerland → watches; Belgium → beer;
    Cuba → cigars)
  - Social/institutional (China → low-cost manufacturing;
    UK → professional services due to skilled workforce)

<!-- page 133: 2 nuggets -->

<!-- page 134: no content (illustration page — Freddo wrapper drawing) -->

---

## Chapter 6: Why aren't Freddos 10p any more?

*On inflation and where it comes from, why everything getting
cheaper might not be as fun as it sounds, and bad Covid-era
haircuts.*

### The Freddo Price Puzzle

- **Freddo** = Cadbury's iconic frog-shaped chocolate bar; once
  cost **10p**; now (at time of writing) **25p** after peaking
  at **30p in 2017** [NICE TO KNOW][N098]
  - 2010: 17p; 2017: 30p; 2021: slight respite; writing: 25p
- The rising Freddo price is a proxy for a broader phenomenon:
  **inflation** — prices across the economy gradually drifting
  upward, eroding the purchasing power of money [MUST KNOW][N099]

<!-- page 135: 2 nuggets -->

### Measuring Inflation: The CPI Basket

- **Inflation** is measured by tracking a **hypothetical
  representative shopping basket** of goods and services:
  bread, milk, shampoo, T-shirts, gym memberships, etc.
  [MUST KNOW][N100]
- If that basket cost £100 a year ago and now costs £102:
  inflation = **2%**
- Not all items move identically — the 2% is an average:
  some items rise fast (UK coach fares +95% since 2010;
  peanuts +70%; Tower of London entry +60%)
  while others fall (men's football boots -20%; computer
  games -30%; laptops -50%+) [SHOULD KNOW][N101]
- UK prices have risen ~**20% cumulatively since 2010**
  (at time of writing)
- In UK, the basket is compiled by the **Office for National
  Statistics (ONS)**; equivalent bodies exist in every country

<!-- page 136: 2 nuggets -->

### Varieties of Inflation Measures

- **Consumer Price Index (CPI)** = most common; captures what
  average household *consumes* [SHOULD KNOW][N102]
- **House prices NOT included** in most headline CPI measures —
  because you don't 'consume' a house; it isn't a regular
  weekly/monthly purchase
  - Exception: UK does include rental costs (standing in for
    mortgage costs) in one headline measure
- **'Cost of living' indices** = alternative measures that
  capture a wider concept, adapting the basket accordingly
- What's in the basket varies by country and era:
  - France: frogs' legs and escargot
  - Germany: bratwurst and schnitzel
  - UK: 'oven-ready joints'
  - 1947 UK basket: gramophone records, cod liver oil
  - 2021 UK basket: Netflix, flavoured milk, condoms, hand
    sanitiser (added in response to Covid)

<!-- page 137: 2 nuggets -->

### Shrinkflation

- **Shrinkflation** = producers reduce product size without
  reducing price → equivalent to a price increase, but harder
  to notice [MUST KNOW][N103]
- 2017: UK ONS identified **206 products** whose size had shrunk
  without price reduction:
  - Fish fingers: 12 → 10 per pack
  - Teabags: 80 → 75 per box
  - Toilet roll: 221 → 200 sheets
- Economically identical to raising the price (you pay same,
  get less; price-per-unit rises)
- Famous example: **Mondelēz** tried to narrow **Toblerone**
  bars in 2016 → furious backlash from consumers → reverted
  to original shape 18 months later
- Freddo also victim: in 2020, Australian consumers saw
  Freddo packs reduced in weight and size with no price change

<!-- page 138: 2 nuggets -->

### Quality Adjustment Problem

- Tracking inflation accurately requires adjusting for quality
  improvements, otherwise you confuse price rises with value
  rises [SHOULD KNOW][N104]
- Example: **Nokia 3310** (2000) cost £129.99 vs
  **iPhone 12** (2020) at £579+ — implied inflation of ~450%
  but the iPhone has camera, internet, apps, speakers, GPS…
  it is a fundamentally different and far more capable product
- Technology is the extreme case: on a **quality-adjusted
  basis**, prices of goods in the UK are roughly what they
  were in **1988**
- Statistical agencies must continually adjust for quality
  to avoid overstating inflation

<!-- page 138 continued: 1 nugget -->

### Why Inflation Is Deeply Personal

- The headline inflation rate is an average; **your personal
  inflation rate** depends on your actual spending pattern
  [SHOULD KNOW][N105]
- If you spend more on transport than average, you face higher
  personal inflation (transport tends to rise more than dining out)
- Survey evidence: people consistently **feel inflation is higher
  than official measures** — possible reasons:
  1. Their basket genuinely differs from the representative one
  2. **Psychological asymmetry**: people notice price rises more
     than price falls (negativity bias)
  3. **Frequency bias**: items bought regularly (groceries)
     are psychologically over-weighted, and groceries tend to
     have higher inflation than other categories

<!-- page 138 continued: 1 nugget -->

### Inflation and Spending Power Over Time

- 2% average inflation (UK/most advanced economies over last
  30 years) sounds modest — but it **erodes roughly half of
  your cash spending power over three decades** [MUST KNOW][N106]
- Real-world example of nominal vs real wages:
  - UK wages rose 20%+ in the decade leading up to 2021 (~2%/yr)
  - But inflation matched wage gains → real purchasing power
    barely increased
  - Japan contrast: wage rises <1% for a decade, BUT inflation
    averaged ~0% → workers still better off in real purchasing
    power terms than the nominal figures suggest
  - **Lower nominal pay rise + lower inflation can be better
    than higher nominal pay rise + matching inflation**
- Inflation matters for pensions, mortgages, savings, salary
  negotiations — any decision that stretches across time

<!-- page 139: 2 nuggets -->

### The Lottery Example: Inflation's Long-Term Bite

- **UK National Lottery** first draw: November 1994
  — jackpot £5,874,778 split among 7 winners; each received
  ~£840,000 [NICE TO KNOW][N107]
- If a winner had put £840,000 in a suitcase in 1994 and
  opened it in 2022: its real value would have **more or less
  halved** due to inflation
- **Set for Life** lottery (modern variant): James Evans,
  21, won £10,000/month in December 2020
  - Family ski trip to Canada costs ~1 month's winnings now
  - By end of his 30-year payout, the same trip would likely
    cost 2 months' winnings → inflation steadily erodes the
    value of a fixed nominal income stream
- Historical perspective: Elizabeth I's 1567 lottery first
  prize was £5,000 — could buy **1,000 horses, 4,000 cows
  or 1.2 million quarts of beer**; £5,000 in 2022 is loose
  change. **Equivalent spending power today ≈ £2.7 million**

<!-- page 139 continued: 1 nugget -->

### Hyperinflation: When Inflation Goes Catastrophic

- **Zimbabwe 2008**: inflation rate of almost **80 billion
  percent** — prices doubled every ~24 hours [MUST KNOW][N108]
- Buying a newspaper in the morning; same headline next day
  but paper costs twice as much
- **'The Great Inflation'** (US and much of world, mid-1960s
  to early 1980s): driven by oil price spikes + government
  spending + central bank policy errors → double-digit annual
  inflation; at those rates, money halves in value every
  **5–7 years**; if sustained 30 years, spending power falls
  to **just 2% of starting value**
- **Hyperinflation** = no hard definition but triple-digit
  year-on-year price rises are a clear signal
- Recurrent force in history: Germany 1920s (wheelbarrows
  of cash to buy bread; contributed to political upheaval
  leading to WWII); more recently Hungary, Greece, Argentina,
  Venezuela, Lebanon
- By some estimates, there has been a hyperinflation episode
  somewhere in the world **on average every two years** for
  the last century [SHOULD KNOW][N109]

<!-- page 139: 2 nuggets (Zimbabwe begins) -->

### The Costs of High Inflation

**Menu costs** [SHOULD KNOW][N110]:
- High inflation forces businesses to update prices
  constantly (daily or hourly)
- Changing price signs and labels costs money and time —
  even with relatively stable inflation these '**menu costs**'
  can be significant
- US supermarket study: menu costs equalled up to
  **one-third of profit margins** for companies involved

**Shoe leather costs** [SHOULD KNOW][N111]:
- High inflation reduces the value of holding cash →
  people hold less cash, investing instead for better returns
- They must make **more frequent trips to the bank** to
  withdraw money as needed
- Named 'shoe leather costs' because you literally wear out
  your shoes walking to the bank and back

**Zimbabwe: government printing costs**:
- Mid-2008: Zimbabwe was running out of paper on which to
  print money — and **couldn't afford to buy more**, because
  the paper cost more than the notes were worth
- New denominations announced so rapidly there was no time
  to procure paper with correct watermarks → old watermarks
  were reused
- A 2008 $25 billion note has a watermark originally intended
  for a $500 bill
- By January 2009: highest denomination = **$100 trillion**
  — which **wouldn't even buy a bus ticket** [MUST KNOW][N112]

<!-- page 140: 4 nuggets -->

### Inflation as a Tax on Savings — and a Subsidy for Debtors

- **Inflation = a tax on keeping money**: just as income tax
  takes a fraction of salary, inflation takes a fraction of
  spending power each period [MUST KNOW][N113]
- But: inflation simultaneously **reduces the real value of
  debts** — beneficial to borrowers, harmful to lenders
- Example: borrow £100 from grandma at 10% interest (repay
  £110 in 12 months); if inflation is 10%, the £110 she
  receives buys exactly what £100 bought before → in real
  terms grandma is no better off; the borrower benefits if
  inflation turns out higher than expected
- This creates a fundamental tension in how inflation affects
  different groups in the economy (chapter continues beyond p.140)

<!-- page 140: 2 nuggets -->

<!-- pp. 139-160 -->

## Chapter Six: Why Are Prices Always Going Up? (continued)

### Hyperinflation — History and Costs

<!-- page 139: 4 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N082] **Hyperinflation** is a recurrent force in world history — most famously
  in **Germany in the 1920s**, contributing to political upheaval that led
  to WWII (wheelbarrows of cash to buy bread). More recently:
  Hungary, Greece, Argentina, Venezuela, Lebanon.
  - Estimate: an instance of hyperinflation somewhere in the world
    on average **every two years** for the last century

- [MUST KNOW][N083] **Menu costs** — the literal cost to businesses of updating prices
  during high inflation (new signs, reprinting, staff time). One study
  on the US supermarket industry found menu costs equal to as much as
  **one-third of profit margins**.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N084] **Shoe leather costs** — high inflation causes people to hold less
  cash and make more frequent trips to the bank to convert assets back
  to cash. Named for the idea you literally wear out your shoes walking
  to and from the bank. Reflects a real efficiency loss in how people
  manage money.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N085] High inflation makes it near impossible to operate a business:
  prices may need updating daily or hourly, not just infrequently.

<!-- page 140: 4 nuggets -->

### Zimbabwe — The Extreme Case

- [MUST KNOW][N086] **Zimbabwe 2008**: In mid-2008, Zimbabwe was running out of paper
  to print new banknotes because the paper cost more than the
  denominations printed on it. New denominations were announced so
  rapidly there was no time to buy paper with the correct watermark —
  old watermarks were reused. A **2008 $25 billion note** had a watermark
  showing the paper had been intended as a $500 bill.
  - By January 2009, the highest-denomination note was **$100 trillion**
    — not enough for a bus ticket.

- [MUST KNOW][N087] **Inflation as a tax on keeping money**: Just as income tax takes a
  fraction of salary, inflation takes a fraction of spending power.
  Critically, **inflation reduces the value of debts in real terms** —
  if you borrow £100 at 10% interest expecting 10% inflation, the
  repayment is painless. If inflation turns out to be 20%, the
  borrower gains; the lender (e.g. Grandma) loses purchasing power.
  - Debts are set in **nominal terms** (a fixed number), not real terms.

- [MUST KNOW][N088] **Inflation as a redistributive force**:
  - Erodes the value of savings (bad for savers)
  - Reduces the real cost of debts (good for borrowers)
  - Tends to shift spending power **from old (asset-holders) to young
    (borrowers)**
  - Tempts debt-laden governments to deliberately engineer inflation to
    reduce their debt burden — but this is paid for by the inflation
    tax on citizens' savings

<!-- page 141: 3 nuggets -->

### Why Not Have Falling Prices? The Case Against Deflation

- [MUST KNOW][N089] **Deflation** seems appealing (cheaper things!) but is deeply
  dangerous. The US Great Depression case: after the 1929 Wall Street
  Crash, prices fell at **10% per year by 1932**. Mechanism:
  1. Demand collapsed (people lost money, held cash)
  2. Businesses cut costs and production
  3. Workers received lower wages or none — unemployment hit **25%**
  4. Those workers were also customers — less income = less demand
  5. Vicious cycle: lower demand → lower prices → lower wages → even
     less demand

- [SHOULD KNOW][N090] **Deflationary spiral**: Once deflation sets in it gains its own
  momentum. If something will be 10% cheaper tomorrow, rational
  consumers delay purchasing (especially big-ticket items: TVs,
  fridges, cars). This permanently deferred demand reduces prices
  further, making it even more rational to delay. "Notoriously
  difficult to drag out of."

<!-- page 142: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N091] **Deflation raises the real cost of debt** — the opposite of
  inflation. If you borrowed £100 and prices fall, that £100 is worth
  more in real terms when you repay it. Deflation compounds debt
  burdens across the whole economy.

- [MUST KNOW][N092] **The optimal target: low, stable positive inflation**
  - High enough that the risk of tipping into deflation during a
    downturn is low
  - Low enough that it doesn't risk spiralling into hyperinflation
  - Most high-income countries target **2% per year**; some economists
    argue for 3-4%
  - Near-universal agreement that costs become particularly severe once
    inflation hits **~10%**, and risk of runaway inflation grows
    rapidly from there

### Covid Haircut Example — Causes of Inflation

<!-- page 143: 3 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N093] UK haircut price example post-Covid: barbers had to buy safety
  equipment, clean equipment between clients, space out customers —
  reducing throughput per chair. In the **18 months after the first
  Covid lockdown**, the price of a haircut rose **more than 8%**.
  Illustrates that inflation arises from millions of individual
  business decisions about costs and demand — not from economic
  theory.

- [MUST KNOW][N094] **Two categories of inflation drivers:**
  1. **Cost-push inflation** — forces that push up costs of inputs,
     forcing businesses to raise prices
  2. **Demand-pull inflation** — forces that increase demand beyond
     the economy's ability to supply, allowing sellers to charge more

- [MUST KNOW][N095] **Four major drivers of cost-push inflation:**
  1. **Commodity price rises** (oil, gas, steel)
  2. **Exchange rate movements**
  3. **Wage increases** (but only when not matched by productivity)
  4. **Taxation**

<!-- page 144: 3 nuggets -->

### Cost-Push: Commodity Prices — The 1973 Oil Shock

- [MUST KNOW][N096] **OPEC oil embargo, October 1973**: OPEC (mostly Middle Eastern
  oil-exporting nations) announced an embargo against western
  countries they accused of supporting Israel in the Arab-Israeli war.
  Over the next **five months, the oil price rose by a factor of three**
  — triggering the wave of inflation that defined the 1970s.
  - Oil is used in virtually everything — energy source, raw material
    (plastics), fuel for transport. A rise in oil prices = a rise in
    the cost of almost all goods.
  - Producers faced two options: absorb the cost (hit profits) or
    pass it on (hit demand). In practice: a mix of both.
  - Result: consumer prices rose **over 14% worldwide in 1974**

### Cost-Push: Exchange Rates

- [SHOULD KNOW][N097] **Exchange rate movements as a driver of inflation**: When your
  currency depreciates, the cost of imported inputs rises immediately.
  Example: Swiss franc depreciated ~10% in September 2011 in a single
  day. Swiss chocolate makers importing German milk found their costs
  instantly 10% higher in franc terms. In a globally integrated
  economy (wood from Canada, tech parts from South Korea, coffee from
  Ecuador), cumulative exchange-rate fluctuations are significant.

<!-- page 145: 2 nuggets -->

### Cost-Push: Wages and Taxation

- [MUST KNOW][N098] **Not all wage increases cause inflation** — the *reason* for the
  wage increase matters:
  - **Productivity-driven pay rises**: worker makes 100 widgets/hr at
    £10/hr (10p/widget); gets 10% pay rise because they now make 110
    widgets/hr. Cost per widget still 10p — no inflation.
  - **Demand-driven pay rises**: company can't find workers, pays more
    to attract them. No productivity gain → cost per unit rises →
    may pass on to consumers.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N099] **Taxation as cost-push inflation**:
  - 2018 UK **sugar tax**: Coca-Cola refused to reformulate, so raised
    prices.
  - January 2011 UK **VAT rise** from 17.5% to 20%: a £25 T-shirt
    gained 50p. Inflation jumped from ~3.5% (Dec 2010) to over **5%
    (Sep 2011)**. Economists estimated that without the VAT rise,
    inflation would have been ~4%.

<!-- page 146: 2 nuggets -->

### Demand-Pull Inflation

- [MUST KNOW][N100] **Demand-pull inflation**: When demand for goods outstrips the
  economy's ability to increase supply, prices rise. Drivers:
  - Consumer confidence ("animal spirits" — **John Maynard Keynes'**
    term for impulsive swings in public mood that drive behaviour)
  - Any factor that causes people to want to spend more than the
    economy can produce

- [MUST KNOW][N101] **Milton Friedman's monetarist claim**: Inflation is
  *"always and everywhere a monetary phenomenon."* Without an increase
  in the money supply relative to output, prices cannot persistently
  rise. Evidence: Friedman and colleague **Anna Schwartz** conducted a
  statistical analysis in the 1950s-60s showing that as money supply
  increased, inflation reliably increased — and vice versa.

<!-- page 147: 3 nuggets -->

### Monetarism and the Quantity Theory of Money

- [MUST KNOW][N102] **Quantity Theory of Money** (Friedman): The amount of money being
  spent must equal the value of things produced, imported, and bought.
  That value = quantity of things × their price. Therefore more money
  in circulation without more real output = higher prices.

- [MUST KNOW][N103] **Long-run neutrality of money**: Monetarists argue the number of
  things an economy produces in the long run is determined by
  fundamental, slow-moving forces (working-age population,
  technological sophistication) — not by how much money is circulating.
  So more money = same real output but higher prices (inflation), not
  more actual economic activity.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N104] **Monetarism in practice**: Became the dominant economic ideology
  in the 1970s-80s — adopted by **Margaret Thatcher** (UK) and
  **Ronald Reagan** (US). Friedman advocated a fixed rate of growth for
  the money supply to achieve constant, stable inflation.

<!-- page 148: 2 nuggets -->

### Keynesians vs. Monetarists

- [MUST KNOW][N105] **The Keynesian vs. Monetarist divide on inflation**:
  - Both agreed that **in the short run**, money creation can boost
    real economic activity (economy doesn't instantly adjust via
    inflation)
  - Monetarists: effects wear off fast → just feeds inflation quickly
  - Keynesians: the adjustment period is long enough to be useful
    ("in the long run we are all dead" — Keynes)
  - The debate continued through the late 20th century.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N106] By the **early 1990s**, consensus tipped away from pure
  monetarism — the empirical relationship between money supply and
  inflation started breaking down. "Suddenly, more money didn't
  necessarily mean more inflation." Causes:
  1. Seismic changes in banking/financial sectors in the 1980s
  2. Policymakers' focus on money supply changed behaviour —
     **Goodhart's Law** (when a measure becomes a target, it ceases
     to be a good measure)
  3. The **velocity of money** turned out not to be stable

<!-- page 149: 3 nuggets -->

### Velocity of Money and Inflation Expectations

- [MUST KNOW][N107] **Velocity of money**: The speed at which money changes hands.
  Even with the same quantity of money in the system, if it circulates
  faster (same pound spent by 10 people per week vs. once), more
  economic activity occurs. Monetarists assumed velocity was relatively
  stable and predictable. Post-1980s evidence showed velocity can
  change a lot and unpredictably — breaking the simple link between
  money supply and inflation.

- [MUST KNOW][N108] **Inflation expectations** (via René Descartes: "I think, therefore
  I am"): What people *believe* will happen to inflation is one of the
  biggest determinants of what *actually* happens. Mechanism:
  - If you expect high inflation, you demand larger pay rises
  - Those wage rises push up costs
  - Which creates the inflation you feared — **self-fulfilling prophecy**
  - Conversely, if people believe inflation will be low, they accept
    low pay rises, keeping costs down and inflation low

<!-- page 150: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N109] **Why explicit inflation targets exist**: Because inflation is
  partly self-fulfilling, central banks (including the **Bank of
  England**) publish explicit inflation targets. If people believe
  policymakers will hit the target, they form expectations consistent
  with that target — which helps *keep* inflation at that level.
  "Oddly, by believing they know what inflation is going to be, they
  keep it at that level."

- [SHOULD KNOW][N110] Chapter 6 conclusion (Freddo framing): A little inflation is
  beneficial — guards against deflationary spirals, encourages
  spending today rather than deferring, and erodes debt burdens over
  time. The concern is when inflation accelerates. The source also
  matters: cost-push vs. demand-pull require different policy
  responses. The debate about money supply's role in driving inflation
  continues.

<!-- page 151: no content -->
<!-- (End of Chapter Six — chapter break illustration) -->

---

## Chapter Seven: What Actually Is Money?

*On what makes money money, why you can't print your own currency,
and how you too could get the sort code 10-00-00.*

<!-- page 152: 4 nuggets -->

### Origins of Money — History and Diversity

- [MUST KNOW][N111] **Money has existed for ~5,000 years** in various forms. Ancient
  **Mesopotamia**: debt systems developed around grain storage —
  workers kept grain in temples/warehouses ("grain banks"). A ledger
  (stone tablet spreadsheet) tracked ownership. You could lend grain
  or exchange claims without physically moving it.
  - This is the same basic premise as modern bank accounts: **money as
    a form of memory** — a way of keeping score of who owes what to
    whom.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N112] **Coins** were invented by the ancient **Lydians** around **700 BCE**:
  casting precious metal into a standard size/shape and stamping it
  with an official mark so people could trust it. A coin by **King
  Croesus of Lydia** (6th century BCE, stamped with lion and bull) is
  in the British Museum. **Paper money** developed more than a
  millennium later, in China.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N113] **Rai Stones of Yap** (Pacific island): Huge stones mined across
  dangerous water. A stone's value was based not just on size but on
  the cost of obtaining it — especially lives lost in transit.
  Particularly lucky (no lives lost) = especially valuable. Like
  Mesopotamian grain banks, the system evolved so the *record of
  ownership* mattered, not physical possession — stones that fell to
  the bottom of the sea still held value because islanders kept
  records of who owned them. Pattern repeated throughout history:
  valuable but cumbersome commodity money replaced by lighter, more
  portable record-keeping.

<!-- page 153: 3 nuggets -->

- [NICE TO KNOW][N114] **Bizarre historical currencies**: Cigarettes in POW camps,
  **mackerel in US prisons** in the early 2000s, **squirrel pelts in
  medieval Russia** (snouts, claws, ears used as change). Demonstrates
  money emerges spontaneously whenever humans need to interact.

- [MUST KNOW][N115] **Three functions of money** (first laid out by **Adam Smith**,
  ~250 years ago — still the standard framework):
  1. **Medium of exchange**: You can pay for things with it; others
     will accept it not because they want to consume it but because
     they can use it to pay for other things. Distinct from **barter**
     (chicken-for-grain: both parties consuming what they receive).
  2. **Store of value**: Money must carry value from now into the
     future — a "time machine for spending power." Bananas fail this
     test; gold passes. Salt, tea bricks, and (as of 2009) Parmesan
     cheese in Italy have all been used historically.
  3. **Unit of account**: A common measuring stick for the price of
     everything. Without it, in a barter economy of 150,000 products,
     you'd need ~**11 billion prices** (every pair of goods). Money
     collapses this to just 150,000 prices: one per good.

<!-- page 154: 2 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N116] **Double coincidence of wants** — the fundamental problem money
  solves. In barter, you need to find someone who wants exactly what
  you have, and has exactly what you want, at the same time. Even in
  tiny societies this is extremely unlikely. Money breaks this: you
  exchange what you produce for money, hold it, then spend it on what
  you want — the seller doesn't need to want what you originally
  produced.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N117] The three functions of money are interdependent:
  - A bad store of value can't be a reliable medium of exchange
    (people won't accept it)
  - A bad medium of exchange can't be a reliable store of value
    (if others won't accept it tomorrow, it can't hold value today)
  - The prevalent medium of exchange naturally becomes the dominant
    unit of account (makes sense to price things in what you pay with)

<!-- page 155: 2 nuggets -->

### Money as Trust

- [MUST KNOW][N118] **Money is fundamentally trust**: The three functions don't fully
  capture what money is. "At its core, money is trust. Money works
  because we all agree, as a society, that it has value... It works
  because we believe it works." — Circular but real. Workers accept
  money because shopkeepers accept it; shopkeepers accept it because
  others will too.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N119] UK banknotes have carried the phrase **"I promise to pay the bearer
  on demand..."** since **1694**. For most of that history it was a
  literal promise to pay the value in gold. Now it means the Bank of
  England guarantees you can exchange it for other Bank of England
  notes — the promise is backed by institutional trust, not a commodity.

### Commodity Money and the Gold Standard

<!-- page 156: 4 nuggets -->

- [MUST KNOW][N120] **Commodity/representative money**: Currency that represents a
  claim on an underlying asset. Historical examples: gold, silver,
  even a **cocoa standard in South America** (prices fixed in cocoa
  beans). Precious metals became dominant because they are hard-wearing
  (durable store of value) and universally valued.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N121] Gold's value is largely circular — people value it because people
  value it. **Thomas More** satirised this in *Utopia* (1516): a
  society where people realise gold isn't particularly useful and make
  it into toilet pans. The uselessness of gold doesn't matter for its
  monetary function, provided people continue to believe in it.

- [MUST KNOW][N122] **The Gold Standard**: In the 1800s the UK and its colonies agreed
  to exchange currency for gold at a specified rate; by end of century
  much of the world had adopted it.
  - Mechanism: economies had to hold enough gold to fulfil currency
    demand → *limits on how much money they could create*
  - Most countries suspended the Gold Standard in **WWI**, returned
    after, then after **WWII** a new US-led global standard developed:
    the **Bretton Woods system** — US promised to convert dollars to
    gold at a fixed rate; other countries pegged their currencies to
    the dollar (effectively pegging to gold). Dollars flowed across
    borders to balance demand differences.

- [SHOULD KNOW][N123] **President Herbert Hoover, 1933**: *"We have gold because we
  cannot trust governments."* — The Gold Standard as a constraint on
  government money-printing. The argument for gold: it anchors
  currency value and prevents governments from inflating away their
  debts.

<!-- page 157: 2 nuggets — partial page, chapter continues -->

- [MUST KNOW][N124] **The fundamental tension in commodity money**: By requiring
  currencies to be backed by a fixed supply of gold, the Gold Standard
  limits money creation and thus constrains both inflation AND the
  ability to respond to economic downturns with more money. This
  tension between "anchor" (price stability) and "flexibility"
  (respond to crises) is the core argument for and against the Gold
  Standard.

<!-- page 158: no content -->
<!-- page 159: no content -->
<!-- page 160: no content -->

<!-- pp. 159-180 -->

## Chapter 7: What Actually Is Money? (continued)

### Money as Trust — The Core Insight

- [MUST KNOW][N082] **Money at its core is trust** — it works because
  we collectively agree it has value. The shopkeeper accepts it because
  they know someone else will. "It works because we believe it works."
- Since 1694, all UK banknotes have included the phrase:
  "I promise to pay the bearer on demand..."
- For most of the last 300 years this was a literal promise to pay
  in **gold** — you could turn up to the Bank of England and demand
  your gold in exchange for the note
- This is **commodity money** / **representative money**: currency
  representing a claim on an underlying asset
- Historical examples of commodity standards: gold, silver, and even
  a **cocoa bean standard** in South America (prices fixed in cocoa
  beans "until surprisingly recently")
- **Alan Turing** became the latest face on UK banknotes in 2021,
  joining Churchill, Jane Austen, Florence Nightingale, Adam Smith

<!-- page 159: 3 nuggets -->

### The Gold Standard

- [MUST KNOW][N083] **The Gold Standard**: system where currencies
  could be converted to gold at a fixed rate
  - 1800s: UK and colonies agreed to exchange currency for gold at
    specified rates; by end of 19th century spread globally
  - Constraint: economies had to hold enough gold to fulfil demand
    for their currency → limited money supply expansion
  - **Bretton Woods** (post-WWII): new global Gold Standard
    underpinned by US dollar; US promised dollars-to-gold at fixed
    rate; other countries pegged to the dollar → dollars (not gold)
    flowed across borders to balance demand differences
- **Thomas More's *Utopia*** satirised gold's arbitrary value:
  described a world where people realised gold isn't useful and
  used it to make toilet pans
- **President Herbert Hoover (1933)**: "we have gold because we
  cannot trust governments" — without the gold requirement,
  governments would be inclined to expand the money supply
  continually, leading to inflation; gold acted as a disciplining
  device, limiting expansion to what new gold reserves could back

<!-- page 160: 3 nuggets -->

### Why the Gold Standard Failed

- [SHOULD KNOW][N084] **Gold Standard's fatal flaw**: changes in
  gold supply and demand were outside policymakers' control →
  couldn't adjust the money supply to stabilise the economy during
  recessions or booms
  - *Goldfinger* film analogy: villain Auric Goldfinger tries to
    poison US gold reserves with radiation to reduce supply and
    boost his own gold's value — illustrates how gold supply shocks
    could destabilise the whole currency system (thwarted by 007)
- **Collapse timeline**:
  - UK left the Gold Standard in **1931**
  - US President **Richard Nixon** officially broke the dollar-gold
    link in **1971**
- After the Gold Standard: "promise to pay the bearer" now means
  exchange for more banknotes — circular but functional

<!-- page 161: 2 nuggets -->

### Fiat Money and Its Advantages

- [MUST KNOW][N085] **Fiat money** ("let it be done" in Latin):
  money with no intrinsic value, deemed money by government decree
  - Far from being a weakness, the authors argue this has benefits:
    1. Supply can vary as demand fluctuates → keeps prices more stable
    2. More efficient: gold locked up backing currency can't be used
       for jewellery or computer chips — better to use something with
       very little intrinsic value, which "won't be missed"
  - **Chartalist theory**: fiat money gets value partly because the
    state allows (or requires) you to pay taxes with it → creates
    a ready-made, reliable demand; everyone has to pay taxes, so
    if taxes require a certain money, people will pass it on knowing
    they can eventually pass it to the government
- Gold's value is also circular: "there is not much inherent in
  gold that gives it value, other than the fact that people tend
  to value it" — zombie apocalypse test: food/machete vs gold bar?

<!-- page 162: 2 nuggets -->

### Three Types of Money in Modern Economies

- [MUST KNOW][N086] **Three forms of money** in most modern economies:
  1. **Banknotes** — created by central banks; a direct claim on
     the state (the "promise to pay"); passed person-to-person;
     anyone in the economy can hold them
  2. **Reserves / narrow money** — electronic money held by banks
     in accounts at the central bank; only accessible to banks and
     financial companies with central bank access; main use is for
     banks to clear inter-bank debts at end of each day
  3. **Bank deposits** — make up **79% of the money supply** and
     **96% of money used by the general public**; NOT a claim on
     the state — an IOU to a **private company** (a bank)
- Core revelation: **most money we use every day is not created
  by the Bank of England** (or Federal Reserve, or ECB) but by
  commercial banks — "they simply write it down on a spreadsheet
  somewhere"

<!-- page 163: 2 nuggets -->

### How Commercial Banks Actually Create Money

- [MUST KNOW][N087] **The loanable funds model** (old textbook view)
  was WRONG: it said banks take in deposits from savers and lend
  them out; this "gets the sequencing the wrong way round"
  - Reality: banks don't need deposits to make a loan — they simply
    add money to your account; on the other side of their books,
    they hold your IOU (the loan) → **matching increase in assets
    and liabilities**; the money in your account "didn't exist five
    seconds ago"
  - This happens every time: mortgage issued, credit card used,
    business loan granted
  - **Converse**: when you repay a loan, the bank cancels your debt
    AND reduces your bank balance → **paying back debt destroys
    money**
- Why banks self-limit:
  - Profit motive: only lend to those likely to repay profitably
  - Demand constraint: borrowers must *want* to take on debt at the
    offered terms; banks can't force anyone to take a loan
  - "At the point banks stop making loans, money creation stops"

<!-- page 164: 2 nuggets -->

### Limits on Bank Money Creation — Regulation

- [SHOULD KNOW][N088] **Regulation constrains money creation**:
  - Banks are strictly regulated; part of a banking licence =
    commitment not to overstretch beyond resources
  - Bank of England's role: regulate how much commercial banks
    can bring into circulation and ensure it's done sustainably
  - Central banks also influence borrowing demand via **interest
    rates** → affects how much households/companies want to borrow
    → indirect control of total money in circulation

<!-- page 165: 1 nugget -->

### Bitcoin and Private Money

- [MUST KNOW][N089] **Bitcoin's origin**: In 2008, **Satoshi
  Nakamoto** (anonymous computer scientist or group) published
  "Bitcoin: a peer-to-peer electronic cash system"
  - Goal: money completely independent from central banks,
    governments, and commercial banks
  - Trust mechanism: **blockchain** — transactions verified
    collectively via checks and balances in the computer code;
    also provides anonymity
  - Total supply **hardwired at 21 million bitcoins** (cap reached
    ~2041); no authority can create more
  - Launched January 2009; the very first block of Bitcoin coded
    in a reference to a *Times* headline: "Chancellor on the brink
    of second bailout for banks" — deliberate anti-bank statement
  - Timing was "impeccable": launched at the moment the 2007-8
    financial crisis was destroying confidence in banks

> "Anyone can create money, the problem is to get it accepted."
> — **Hyman Minsky**, American economist

<!-- page 165-166: 2 nuggets -->

### Why Bitcoin Fails as Money (Economists' View)

- [MUST KNOW][N090] **Bitcoin fails all three of Adam Smith's
  functions of money**:
  1. **Store of value FAIL**: Bitcoin is 10x more volatile than
     most major exchange rates — if someone owed you £100 and
     offered a piece of paper that tomorrow could be £200 or 10p,
     "would you take it? Maybe for a gamble, but not for your
     day-to-day spending"
  2. **Unit of account FAIL**: few people set prices in Bitcoin;
     stores that accept it work from an exchange rate back to fiat
     currencies; even in **El Salvador** (Bitcoin became official
     legal tender 2021), prices are listed alongside US dollars,
     "which have proven themselves to be a far more stable way of
     storing your purchasing power"
  3. **Medium of exchange FAIL**: transactions much slower than
     traditional systems; can't handle global payment volumes;
     massive energy cost — mining requires "millions of computer
     servers crunching billions of numbers using terrawatts of
     electricity"
- **Fixed supply = Gold Standard problem**: rigid supply makes it
  impossible to react to demand changes over time → significant
  price volatility; "Bitcoin is undoubtedly a fascinating
  technology... but most economists agree that it isn't money itself"

<!-- page 167: 2 nuggets -->

### History of Private Money Failures

- [SHOULD KNOW][N091] **Private money consistently fails without
  state backing** — Bitcoin is only the latest example:
  - **US early 1800s "free banks"**: >90% of notes in circulation
    in 1830 were private bank notes; people didn't trust notes from
    banks beyond their local area, making it hard to travel;
    system ultimately simplified to a single nationally dictated
    banknote form
  - **UK local community currencies** (Bristol, Brixton): designed
    to keep money in local economies; fail as medium of exchange
    because suppliers outside the area won't accept them —
    a Bristol deli owner buying sausages from Cumberland, turkeys
    from Norfolk, and beef from the Highlands cannot pay in
    Bristol pounds; "your suppliers won't accept payment in Bristol
    pounds because they can't spend it in their area"
  - **Scottish banknotes**: privately issued but must be backed
    one-for-one by money at the Bank of England; Scottish banks
    hold special notes at the Bank of England: "**Giants**" (£1
    million each) and "**Titans**" (£100 million each); yet
    Scottish notes still face suspicion from people in other parts
    of the UK — even though "they are, in theory, more securely
    backed by the British state than deposits in your bank account"

<!-- page 168: 2 nuggets -->

### The Complex Nature of Trust Around Money

- Money is "ultimately a system of mutually agreed trust"; over
  the centuries people have reinforced that trust by:
  1. Drawing on the intrinsic value of precious metals/commodities
  2. Drawing on the resources and power of the state
  3. Drawing on the "cool logic of computer code" (Bitcoin)
  — "Sometimes these methods work, sometimes they don't. But in
  every case the goal — explicit or implicit — has been to
  reinforce people's faith that the money really does have value"
- Scottish banknotes case shows trust is socially constructed
  even when the underlying backing is solid

<!-- page 169: 1 nugget -->

### The Future of Money: Central Bank Digital Currency (CBDC)

- [SHOULD KNOW][N092] **Central Bank Digital Currency (CBDC)**:
  technology now makes it feasible for central banks to offer
  accounts to everyone in the economy
  - Context: UK cash use fallen **70% in a decade**, ~50% in last
    three years; digital payments dominate but require private
    bank money (IOU system)
  - Bank of England staff once had internal accounts (sort code
    **10-00-00**); closed in 2017; CBDC would revive this for
    everyone
  - **Potential benefit 1 — interest-bearing**: unlike physical
    cash, a CBDC could pay interest; **Milton Friedman** identified
    the inefficiency of holding paper money (no interest earned
    while inflation erodes purchasing power); his 1970s solution
    was to target zero inflation; CBDC removes the problem
    directly by letting central bank set interest rate on digital
    balances
  - **Potential benefit 2 — state-backed digital payments**:
    currently digital payments can only be made with private bank
    money; CBDC allows digital payments directly via state-backed
    money without relying on commercial bank IOUs
  - **Key risk**: if everyone holds money in CBDC accounts, no
    one holds deposits with commercial banks → could destabilise
    the banking sector, which "performs a crucial role in keeping
    the economy running smoothly" (topic of next chapter)
- Money has "always evolved with the times, and it will continue
  to do so. It meets the needs and desires of the society it serves"

<!-- page 170-173: 3 nuggets -->

### Chapter 7 Conclusion: What Is Money?

- [MUST KNOW][N093] **Three-level answer to "what is money?"**:
  1. **Anything we all agree it is** — gold coins, banknotes,
     precious rocks, old teeth, cigarettes, mackerels
  2. **Functionally**: must fulfil Adam Smith's three criteria —
     medium of exchange, store of value, unit of account; coins
     are a better vehicle than mackerel on these criteria
  3. **In the modern world**: largely a system of IOUs issued by
     private companies (banks), not a claim on the state or gold;
     "The bulk of the money we use every day, in the form of bank
     deposits, is nothing more than a promise by our bank to pay
     us back, created out of thin air when they lend"
- Trust maintained by: (1) central banks, (2) commercial banks,
  (3) state regulation and monitoring of money creation "to ensure
  it is undertaken responsibly"
- Banks also "bring the potential for great economic fragility" —
  sets up Chapter 8

<!-- page 174: 1 nugget -->

<!-- page 175: no content (chapter illustration — mattress with money) -->

---

## Chapter 8: Why Shouldn't I Hoard All My Money Under the Mattress?

*On the benefits of banking, why your money should be safer in a bank
than ever before, and how to avoid accidentally binning all your savings.*

### Opening Anecdote and Cash-Hoarding Psychology

- [SHOULD KNOW][N094] **Israeli mattress story (2009)**: woman
  surprised her mother with a new mattress; the old one — thrown
  away at a Tel Aviv tip — contained **$1 million in cash**
  (the mother's life savings, hoarded after distrust of banks
  following the 2007-8 financial crisis); daughter searched
  three tips for a week; money was lost
- Cash hoarding is widespread and historically persistent:
  - Covid-19 (2020): **nearly 1 in 10 people** started hoarding
    extra cash (Bank of England survey)
  - 2020: archaeologists discovered 1,069 silver coins in Suffolk,
    believed hidden during the English Civil War (17th century)
  - **2018 YouGov survey**: two-thirds of people said they didn't
    trust banks
- Root cause: a very normal human instinct, especially during
  economic turmoil; "many of us know older relatives who keep
  large amounts of cash... in a drawer in the kitchen or back
  bedroom"

<!-- page 176-177: 2 nuggets -->

### History of Banking

- [SHOULD KNOW][N095] **Banking origin — Babylonian temple priests
  (~2000 BCE)**:
  - Priests stored people's gold for a fee (fraction of gold
    stored); gold sat idle in temple basements "gathering dust
    alongside statues of gods and goddesses"
  - By 1800 BCE: priests began lending out stored gold to others
    who needed it — realising most depositors didn't need it all
    the time
  - "These business-savvy priests didn't realise it, but they had
    created what is now perhaps the most important business in
    the world. These temples were arguably the world's first banks."
  - Model spread to Greece, then Rome; collapsed with the Roman
    Empire; dormant for almost a millennium

- [SHOULD KNOW][N096] **Medieval banking revival — Lombard
  moneylenders (12th-13th century, northern Italy)**:
  - "**Banks**" etymology: *banca* = Italian for bench; small
    wooden benches where moneylenders operated on the streets
    of Lombardy
  - "**Bankruptcy**" etymology: *banca rotta* = "rotten bench";
    when a moneylender ran out of money, the bench was broken
    in half and business ceased
  - Initially lent to farmers buying grain; charged interest
    (compensated by share of harvest) because of default risk
  - Soon also held deposits — initially as emergency reserves
    if harvest failed, but often used for the bench's own trades
  - By 15th century: deposits in exchange for a bill of exchange
    (precursor to the banknote)
  - By 17th century: banks across Europe — **Barclays** (England),
    **Berenberg** (Germany)
  - Many Lombard benches evolved into banks for European royals
    needing to finance wars
  - Some became "central banks": **Bank of England** founded **1694**
    to offer loans to the state
  - Basic model — institution offering loans backed by financial
    reserves — "has remained largely unchanged since the sixteenth
    century"

<!-- page 178: 2 nuggets -->

### Three Core Functions of Modern Banks

- [SHOULD KNOW][N097] **Function 1: Storing money**
  - UK average person (2021): >£5,000 in bank savings accounts
  - Bank of England underground vaults (Threadneedle Street):
    ~400,000 gold bars worth >£200 billion = ~**one-fifth of all
    gold in the world**; stacked height = 46 Eiffel Towers;
    covers 300,000+ sq ft (≈10 football pitches)
  - Storage function pioneered by Babylonian priests in 2000 BCE

- [SHOULD KNOW][N098] **Function 2: Enabling payments**
  - UK: >£8 million in transactions per second; **£700 billion**
    of bank account exchanges every single day
  - Banks don't transfer money in real time — they keep tallies
    and **settle** what they owe each other at end of day via the
    Bank of England (**settlement**)
  - In 2020, Bank of England processed nearly **£92 trillion** of
    payments
  - Economic benefit: "if banks didn't provide a way for you to
    pay others, it could cause the economy to grind to a halt"

- [SHOULD KNOW][N099] **Function 3: Matching borrowers to savers**
  — solving **asymmetric information**
  - In principle, you could borrow from friends/family; in practice:
    (a) you may not know anyone with large spare cash, and (b) even
    rich friends may not lend — they don't know if you'll repay
  - **Asymmetric information**: borrower knows more about their
    own creditworthiness than the lender; banks are specialists
    at evaluating this risk (a form of market failure introduced
    in Ch. 2)

<!-- page 179-180: 3 nuggets -->

<!-- pp. 179-200 -->

## Chapter 8: Why Do We Need Banks? (continued)

<!-- page 179: covered in previous batch — storing money (N097) -->
<!-- page 180: covered in previous batch — payments/settlement (N098),
     matching borrowers/savers begins (N099) -->

### How Banks Solve Asymmetric Information

- [MUST KNOW][N100] **Banks as specialized information processors
  for credit risk**
  - Asymmetric information in lending: you know your own
    creditworthiness better than any lender — classic market
    failure (introduced Ch. 2 using second-hand car example)
  - Historical evolution of credit assessment:
    - 20th century: man in suit interviews you, asks your
      friends and family about character
    - Today: computers analyze gym memberships, pub spending
      habits, monthly income/outgoings — vast behavioral data
  - Banks have structural informational advantage: they see
    your complete financial history in ways no individual lender
    could; this makes them "more efficient lenders than any
    individual could ever be"
  - Banks specialize in distinguishing good loans from bad —
    this expertise is a competitive moat and social function
  - Interest rate = price of the bank's risk assessment:
    higher default probability → higher rate; "the bigger the
    risk, the higher the interest"

<!-- page 181: 1 nugget (N100) -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N101] **Lending's wider social benefit: idle money
  flows to productive uses**
  - Common misconception: bank lending benefits only borrower
    and bank; this massively underestimates lending's economic
    importance
  - Mechanism: banks match idle savings with productive
    investment → put money "to use in the most productive
    way possible"
  - Fortuitous alignment: the borrowers MOST LIKELY to repay
    loans tend to BE the borrowers who invest productively
    — they generate returns sufficient to service the debt
  - Banks thus "inadvertently" allocate capital efficiently
    while screening for creditworthiness — positive externality
    baked into the credit process
  - "Matching savers to borrowers is a socially important task,
    as well as a way of making money"

<!-- page 182: 1 nugget (N101); goldsmith story begins -->

### The Goldsmith Innovation: Origin of Money Creation

- [MUST KNOW][N102] **17th-century London goldsmiths: how banks
  learned to create money**
  - Context: rising political tensions between King Charles I
    and Parliament → wealthy Londoners needed secure gold storage
  - Goldsmiths offered vault storage + issued receipts showing
    quantity and purity of deposited gold; receipt = claim on
    stored gold
  - **Key innovation**: goldsmiths realized they could lend out
    deposited gold for a fee, provided it was returned before
    the original owner wanted it back; gave fee-share to
    depositors as compensation for default risk
  - Why this matters — the multiplier mechanism:
    1. Goldsmith lends gold → borrower invests → generates return
    2. Return reinvested elsewhere → more economic activity
    3. Some returned proceeds deposited with goldsmith again
    4. Goldsmith lends out again → cycle continues
    5. Result: "more money being loaned led to more economic
       activity, which led to more money being loaned" →
       MORE money in the system than originally deposited
  - Modern banks do the same without gold constraint — create
    money "out of thin air" (covered Ch. 7)
  - Both goldsmiths and modern banks perform **maturity
    transformation**: short-term deposits (withdrawable anytime)
    → long-term loans; this mismatch is fundamental to banking
  - "Through this process, banks benefit themselves, savers,
    borrowers and others in the economy – leading to more and
    more productive economic output"

<!-- page 183: 1 nugget (N102) -->

- [MUST KNOW][N103] **Fractional reserve banking: the system
  and its inherent tension**
  - Problem: if goldsmiths lend too much, they can't return
    gold when owners ask for it back
  - Goldsmiths' solution (still used today): keep a **fraction**
    of deposits in reserve at all times → **fractional reserve
    banking**
  - In 17th century: ensured late returns or unexpected early
    withdrawal requests could always be honored
  - Today: all banks keep certain amount of real or virtual
    cash in vaults to limit risk of running out of money
  - Core tension: by definition, a fractional reserve bank has
    LESS cash than total deposits — fine under normal conditions
    (not everyone withdraws simultaneously), catastrophic if
    confidence breaks
  - "The big question, then, is how much cash banks should
    have to hold" — this question has generated major policy
    debate since the 1930s

<!-- page 184: 2 nuggets (N103 + Chicago Plan begins) -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N104] **The Chicago Plan (1930s): radical
  alternative to fractional reserve banking**
  - Early 1930s: six American economists at University of
    Chicago proposed dramatic reform — banks should hold 100%
    of deposits at all times ("full reserve banking")
  - Rationale: fractional reserve is inherently fragile because
    it creates an irreconcilable tension between two bank roles:
    (a) creating money that circulates in the economy and
    (b) allocating credit from savers to borrowers
  - Fatal vulnerability: if ALL depositors demand their money
    simultaneously → entire system collapses — not just one
    bank but the process by which money enters circulation
  - This fragility was "laid bare by the economic crash of 1929"
    (Chicago economists' view)
  - Chicago Plan solution: **separate the two functions**
    - Money creation: leave to government
    - Credit allocation: leave to banks (as pure intermediaries)
    - Banks become "narrow" banks — no gap between deposits
      they hold and loans outstanding; hold enough with central
      bank to cover all potential withdrawals
  - Status: gained traction; still discussed by IMF and academic
    economists; **never became law** — fortunately, people
    don't all demand their money back at once in practice

<!-- page 185: 1 nugget (N104 continued) -->

- [MUST KNOW][N105] **The banking paradox: trust is the
  foundation of the entire system**
  - "The modern banking system rests on a paradox": we all
    believe banks keep our money safe, and for the most part
    they do
  - BUT: if for a moment everyone simultaneously stopped
    believing banks were safe and tried to withdraw money,
    the whole system would collapse
  - Connects to Ch. 7's money trust dynamic: "the very act
    of trusting that the banking system works, makes it work"
  - Bank runs are not irrational — they reflect a logical
    response to a real structural vulnerability
  - **It's a Wonderful Life** (1946 film): George Bailey's
    Bailey Building and Loan faces a run; depositors have
    heard rumors it borrowed from another bank because
    borrowers couldn't pay; George explains honestly:
    deposits are loaned out to other customers — he cannot
    give everyone their money back simultaneously
  - The fictional scenario perfectly illustrates the real
    mechanism: rumors → rational withdrawal → self-fulfilling

<!-- page 185: 1 nugget (N105) -->

### Bank Runs: Self-Fulfilling Prophecies

- [MUST KNOW][N106] **Northern Rock 2007: anatomy of a
  real-world bank run**
  - September 2007: BBC News reported Northern Rock was
    seeking emergency loan from Bank of England due to
    financial problems → queues immediately formed outside
    branches across the UK
  - **Within three days**: Northern Rock became the first
    UK bank run since the 19th century
  - Rational behavior mechanism: even a very small chance
    the bank cannot pay creates a strong incentive to be
    FIRST to withdraw
    - "The longer you wait, the more you think you risk
      losing everything"
    - Every person who joins the queue increases the actual
      probability the bank will run out — self-reinforcing
    - Individual decisions collectively became "a
      self-fulfilling prophecy"
  - WHY it was actually rational in 2007: deposit insurance
    (**Financial Services Compensation Scheme**) only
    guaranteed £2,000 plus 90% of the next £33,000 — people
    with large savings stood to lose substantial sums
  - Post-reform: FSCS now guarantees **all deposits up to
    £85,000** regardless of bank failure — greatly reduces
    incentive to run
  - Historical scale of bank runs: during the Great
    Depression, **7,000 US banks collapsed between 1929
    and 1933**, many triggered by bank runs

<!-- page 186: N106 begins -->
<!-- page 187: N106 continued (self-fulfilling, deposit insurance) -->
<!-- page 188: N106 conclusion + N107 begins -->

### The Lender of Last Resort

- [MUST KNOW][N107] **Bagehot's rule: the conditions for
  central bank emergency lending**
  - When facing a bank run, commercial banks can turn to
    their own bank: in the UK, the Bank of England
  - Central banks are "lenders of last resort" — there for
    banks when they lack cash to pay depositors
  - **Walter Bagehot** — Victorian polymath, editor of
    *The Economist*, author of *The English Constitution* —
    wrote the definitive text on central banking:
    ***Lombard Street*** (named for the London street where
    **Overend, Gurney and Company**, the largest moneylender
    in the City of London, was located)
  - **Overend Gurney crisis, May 1866**:
    - Overend Gurney suspended payments → panic; *The Times*
      dubbed it "Black Friday" (the first of several in
      financial history)
    - Bank of England in 1866 was still private but held
      central bank privileges (gold reserve control, banknote
      monopoly in London)
    - Bank of England **refused to rescue Overend Gurney**:
      concluded the company's woes stemmed from bad business
      decisions and excessive risk-taking — not salvageable
    - Instead, Bank of England supported OTHER banks and
      brokers, depleting its own reserves → prevented
      systemic collapse
    - Bagehot praised this as the correct approach
  - **Bagehot's dictum** (summarized by former BoE Deputy
    Governor **Paul Tucker**):
    > "to avert panic, central banks should lend early and
    > freely (i.e. without limit), to solvent businesses,
    > against good collateral, and at 'high rates'"
  - Unpacking the rule:
    - *Solvent* businesses only: lend to banks that will
      survive long-term and can repay; don't rescue
      fundamentally broken banks
    - *High rates*: discourage banks from borrowing
      unnecessarily from the central bank; only use when
      absolutely necessary
    - *Early and freely*: act fast to prevent panic spreading
  - Analogy: "central banks are a little like economic seat
    belts" — protect against external shocks and unexpected
    storms, but cannot protect against reckless behavior;
    "central banks will only tide over a struggling bank for
    a short period of time"

<!-- page 189: N107 begins -->
<!-- page 190: N107 conclusion + N108 -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N108] **Moral hazard in banking: risk
  compensation and the Peltzman effect**
  - **Moral hazard**: if banks know the central bank will
    bail them out, they have incentive to take excessive risks
    in pursuit of profit — private gain, socialized loss
  - Analogy from economist **Samuel Peltzman** (University
    of Chicago): argued mandatory seat belts would INCREASE
    car accidents because drivers would feel protected and
    take more risks ("risk compensation")
  - Peltzman turned out to be wrong in the UK: road injuries
    fell immediately after compulsory seat belts introduced
    in 1983
  - BUT the moral hazard concern has genuine relevance in
    banking: "If banks knew they had a central bank on
    standby to help them out without strict conditions, why
    wouldn't they take a few more risks?"
  - **Gordon Tullock** proposed "Tullock spikes" — require
    a large spike in the center of the steering wheel instead
    of seat belts — making drivers viscerally aware of danger;
    forces skin in the game
  - Banking equivalent: central banks mitigate moral hazard
    via (a) conditions on emergency lending (collateral,
    high rates) and (b) **banking regulations and supervision**
    — rules banks must follow to be allowed to operate; limits
    on lending, capital requirements

<!-- page 190: N108 continues; p191 N108 conclusion + N109 -->

### Bank Capital: The Key Regulatory Buffer

- [MUST KNOW][N109] **Bank capital: definition, purpose,
  and post-2008 reforms**
  - **Bank capital** = difference between what a bank OWNS
    (assets: loans, investments) and what it OWES (liabilities:
    deposits)
  - Source: shareholders (equity); buy £100 of bank shares →
    provide £100 of capital; losses reduce your stake; profits
    pay dividends
  - Purpose: protective buffer ensuring bank can absorb loan
    losses without becoming insolvent
  - Regulations require banks to set aside capital against
    every loan — "emergency funds" calibrated to loan risk
  - Pre-2008 consensus: **banks had too little capital** —
    view shared by economist **Joseph Stiglitz** and former
    US president **Barack Obama**, among virtually all others
  - Post-2008 reform result: UK banks now hold **more than
    3× as much capital** as before the 2007-8 crisis
  - Critical tradeoff: too-high capital requirements → banks
    stop lending → economy suffers; too-low → banks can't
    absorb losses → financial crises; no perfect answer

<!-- page 191: N109 -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N110] **Macro-prudential policy and the
  countercyclical capital buffer**
  - Post-2008 innovation: capital requirements should be
    **flexible**, not fixed — "macro-prudential policies"
    that adjust to economic and credit conditions
  - **Countercyclical capital buffer (CCB)**: the principle
    that capital requirements should move opposite to the
    credit cycle
    - Credit conditions GOOD (boom, banks lending freely):
      hold MORE capital → build buffer for future shock
    - Credit conditions BAD (bust, banks reluctant to lend):
      hold LESS capital → free up ability to keep lending
  - Logic of CCB: in downturns, if forced to hold too much
    capital, banks would cut lending, making recession worse;
    lowering requirements lets banks keep credit flowing
  - Variable by risk: riskier loans → more capital required;
    safer loans → less; "like speed limits, changing from
    road to road"
  - UK institution: **Financial Policy Committee** (Governor
    + senior economists inside and outside Bank of England)
    sets the CCB buffer size; reviews whenever conditions
    change
  - **COVID-19 example** (March 2020): FPC reduced required
    capital holdings → helped banks continue lending during
    economic shock even as loan repayments grew uncertain
  - **Stress tests**: regulators model specific economic
    scenarios (e.g., "GDP falls 10% — how does your lending
    and capital change?"; "unemployment rises 5pp — what
    are your mortgage losses?") → banks must demonstrate
    resilience → informs capital requirement decisions

<!-- page 192: 1 nugget (N110) -->
<!-- page 193: N110 conclusion + N111 -->

### Regulation, Supervision, and Resolution

- [SHOULD KNOW][N111] **Banking regulation infrastructure
  and the resolution mechanism**
  - Central banks don't just set rules — they have "financial
    traffic officers" to enforce them:
    - **Prudential Regulation Authority (PRA)** — part of
      Bank of England: supervises banks' adherence to capital
      and other prudential rules
    - **Financial Conduct Authority (FCA)** — independent
      body: also polices rule compliance
    - Sanctions: heavy fines; extreme cases — revocation of
      operating license
  - **Resolution**: when banks DO fail, there is now a process
    to ensure they "do so safely without harming customers,
    other banks or the rest of the financial system" —
    orderly wind-down rather than chaotic collapse with
    contagion
  - All these changes are "relatively new" — implemented
    in the wake of 2007-8 crisis when policymakers realized
    the banking sector needed to be more resilient
  - Net result: "your money should be safer in a bank now
    than ever before — and certainly safer than if you
    hoarded it under that mattress"
  - Chapter conclusion: banks are integral to the global
    economy — store money safely, enable payments, channel
    savings to productive uses; but financial crises still
    happen and cannot be fully eliminated → topics for
    final two chapters

<!-- page 194: N111 conclusion and chapter wrap-up -->

---

## Chapter 9: Why Didn't Anyone See It Coming?

### How Often Do Crises Occur?

- [SHOULD KNOW][N100] **Frequency of economic crises across a lifetime**
  - Over an 85-year life, you're likely to live through **eight economic crises**
    — double the number of times the average Briton moves house
  - Authors witnessed two in their own short careers at the
    **Bank of England**
  - No two crises are the same: fallout from 1929 ≠ 1987; thirteen
    stock-market crashes in the US since, each different
  - Shape varies widely: gradual expansion then contraction; or
    sudden tank followed by almost immediate recovery

<!-- page 199: 1 nugget -->

### Causes of Financial Crises

- [MUST KNOW][N101] **Triggers of economic crises — wide variety, no
  single cause**
  - Speculative financial bubbles, bank runs, interest rate rises,
    house price falls, trade wars, oil price changes, wars, famines,
    social unrest, pandemics — or any combination
  - Financial crises are particularly common; oldest recorded:
    **ancient Rome, 33 CE** — price of land fell, landowners who had
    secured loans against land couldn't repay, lenders stopped loaning
  - In recent decades, financial system problems have wider
    implications than before because financial markets are more
    integrated into the rest of the economy — issues ripple outward

### South Sea Bubble (1720) — The Classic Speculative Bubble

- [MUST KNOW][N102] **South Sea Bubble mechanics and lessons**
  - **South Sea Company** founded **1711** to trade with Spanish colonies
    in South America — primarily enslaved Africans in mines/plantations
  - Britain at war with Spain → government had massive debt; solution:
    sell government debt to South Sea Company, issue shareholders
    shares; government pays annual interest fees to shareholders
  - Company **bribed ministers and MPs** to secure the arrangement
  - By 1720: company owned the government's entire **£30 million** of debt
  - Fundamental flaw: South Seas were Spanish/Portuguese colonies —
    hard to generate income; transatlantic slave trade was also
    horrifically risky (high mortality rates = loss of "property" =
    loss of profits); far too risky to secure government finance
  - Despite this, shares soared to nearly **£1,000** (≈£200,000 today)
  - Investors included the **King's mistress (Duchess of Kendal)**
    and **Thomas Pitt, Earl of Londonderry** — aristocrats to
    lower-middle-class Londoners all bought in
  - Some knowingly bought overpriced shares planning to sell before
    the drop; others borrowed from banks; others invested life savings
  - Share value suddenly plummeted — sudden realisation the company
    wasn't worth close to the price being paid

<!-- page 200: 1 nugget -->

### Minsky Moments and Herd Behaviour

- [MUST KNOW][N103] **Minsky moment — the tipping point of speculative
  excess**
  - Named after **Hyman Minsky** (Ch. 7); his work was semi-forgotten
    until rediscovered in the fallout of the **2007–8 financial crisis**
  - Definition: the moment when everyone realises the asset they
    invested in is worth a lot less than they thought — the tipping
    point when speculative activity reaches an unsustainable extreme
  - A particular event triggers initial price fall → panic → more selling
  - South Sea Bubble trigger: realisation the company wasn't
    particularly profitable; investors staring in dismay at
    blackboards in London coffee houses
  - **Isaac Newton** lost ~£20,000 (>£4 million today); he "could
    calculate the motions of the heavenly bodies, but not the
    madness of people"

> "I can calculate the motions of the heavenly bodies, but not the
> madness of people." — Isaac Newton, on losing money in the South
> Sea Bubble

- [SHOULD KNOW][N104] **Herd behaviour** — people making decisions
  based on the behaviour/choices of others, not independent analysis
  - Newton's quote describes it: even the greatest minds can be swept
    up when social pressure overrides rational calculation

<!-- page 201: 2 nuggets -->

### Contagion from Bubbles

- [SHOULD KNOW][N105] **How asset bubbles spill into wider economic
  crises (and when they don't)**
  - South Sea collapse: banks in London went bust as debts went unpaid
    → deleterious effect on prices of other stocks
  - Same mechanism in **1929, 1987, 2007–8**
  - **Bubble anatomy**: massive investment in an asset because people
    think it will go up in value → realisation it's overvalued →
    bubble bursts
  - **Counterexample — Great Beanie Baby Bubble (late 1990s):**
    Soft toys usually worth ~$5; collectors believed they'd keep
    rising → one sold on eBay for >$5,000; smuggling rings formed;
    "Beanie Baby Bandit" (77-year-old) stole $1,200 of toys;
    children hurt in stampede for 'Garcia' bear; manufacturer **Ty**
    briefly became one of the world's biggest toy companies and its
    founder the 877th richest man in the world
  - In 2000, collectors realised toys worth far less → cascade selling
    → one family lost **$100,000**
  - **Why it didn't cause a wider crisis**: only a small number of
    investors; not linked to other parts of the financial system
  - Lesson: bubbles only cause economy-wide crises when they're
    connected to the broader financial system (credit, banks, housing)

<!-- page 202: 1 nugget -->

### Irrational Exuberance and Black Swans

- [MUST KNOW][N106] **Irrational exuberance — why speculative behaviour
  is so widespread**
  - **Richard Shiller** won a Nobel Prize for explaining this:
    as prices increase, investors want to get in on the action
    even if not rational — a phenomenon he named **irrational exuberance**
  - Mechanism: if all your friends rave about an investment
    (e.g. crypto) and say it will only go up, it's difficult to
    believe they're all wrong → you follow their lead
  - At the same time: rising prices make people more confident →
    less risk-averse → they double down → prices rise further
  - Self-reinforcing loop explains why bubbles inflate far beyond
    fundamental value

- [SHOULD KNOW][N107] **Black swan events** — unpredictable catastrophic
  events that are rare and hard to predict in advance
  - **Covid-19** was a classic black swan: national lockdowns in 90+
    countries, >half global population affected; stock market fell
    nearly **10% on 12 March 2020** — worst single-day drop since
    2008 crash
  - Lack of opportunity to spend + reluctance to spend = perfect storm
  - During 2020 alone: **global GDP fell nearly 4%** — likely the
    biggest contraction since the Great Depression
  - Implication: speculation is far from the only cause of crises;
    pandemics, wars, commodity shortages all make the economy harder
    to function

<!-- page 203: 2 nuggets -->

### The Cost of Economic Crises

- [SHOULD KNOW][N108] **Financial cost of crises — and their uneven
  distribution**
  - 2007–8 crisis cost every American approximately **$70,000**;
    every Briton **£21,000** — enough to buy every UK person a new
    Ford Fiesta with change left over
  - But averages mislead: crisis costs fall disproportionately on
    different people depending on what triggered it and what
    policies were implemented
  - Some impacts appear immediately; others not apparent until years
    or decades later

<!-- page 204: 1 nugget -->

### Three Recurrent Consequences of Crises

- [MUST KNOW][N109] **Consequence 1: Fall in confidence → fall in
  spending**
  - Most immediate economic impact: lower confidence → people less
    likely to spend
  - **Alan Greenspan's underwear index**: men put off buying new
    underwear in downturns → underwear sales fall; former Fed chair
    used this as a gauge of economic health
  - Mechanism: falling asset prices (homes) make people feel less
    wealthy → they spend less even when they could
  - Cascades: businesses earn less → can't pay wages/bills →
    may cut wages or shut → workers spend less elsewhere →
    harder for other businesses to survive
  - **Iceland 2009**: GDP fell 8%; Icelandic krona lost 50% of its
    value in a year; McDonald's couldn't import beef/cheese/vegetables
    at viable cost → all **three Reykjavik McDonald's closed
    permanently**; last remaining Big Mac preserved in an
    airtight glass case in a museum in South Iceland (still
    livestreamed today)
  - UK 2007–8 victims: **HMV** (records), **MFI** (furniture),
    **Woolworths** (Pic'n'Mix)

<!-- page 205: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N110] **Consequence 2: Unemployment**
  - When businesses shut, people lose jobs — scaled across an economy,
    this is disastrous
  - Great Depression: **one in four American workers** unemployed
  - Hard to find new job during a recession when everyone is cutting
  - Unequal distribution: **younger and lower-skilled workers hit
    hardest**; workers aged 18–30 experienced higher unemployment
    AND larger falls in real wages than any other group in 2007–8
  - Long-term scarring: unemployed for long periods → permanently
    lose skills → less employable for decades
  - Extreme: lower-skilled workers may stop looking entirely —
    chances of a job are slim and potential wages are low
  - Post-crisis retraining: some out-of-work bankers after 2007–8
    retrained to become **science teachers**

<!-- page 206: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N111] **Consequence 3: Greater inequality**
  - Recessions grow income inequality and spread poverty
  - Post-2008 (2009–10): top 1% saw incomes rise by nearly **12%**;
    remaining 99% saw incomes stay roughly the same — only top
    earners benefited from recovery
  - Post-1929: between 1920 and 1928, the income share of the
    **top 5%** rose from just over a quarter to more than a third
  - Why: those already worse off have less education → fewer job
    options if unemployed; also fewer savings to cushion the fall
  - **Intergenerational inequality**: after 2008–9, the % of British
    children earning ≥ their parents **declined** → strengthened
    parent–child earnings correlation → **inheritance** became a
    more important factor in wealth accumulation
  - Long-run: recessions undermine future generations' **social
    mobility**, which itself slows economic growth

<!-- page 207: 1 nugget -->

### Non-Economic Consequences of Recessions

- [SHOULD KNOW][N112] **Health and social effects of recessions**
  - Mental health: after 2007–8, as unemployment rose, **male
    suicide in the UK increased by more than 15%**
  - Physical health: heart attacks and strokes increase during
    downturns (clear evidence)
  - Children: growing up during economic instability → greater risk
    of developing mental health issues; during last recession,
    children's need for mental-health services increased **by half**
  - Divorce: rates tend to increase after recessions (financial
    strain); 2003–9 downward trend in divorces, then 2010 rose
    **5%**; paradox — people also seek first dates more when the
    economy is troubled ("economic turbulence makes people lonely")
  - Positive side effects: during Covid-19, collapse in Chinese
    manufacturing led to **sharp fall in air pollution** → some
    reduction in city deaths; during Great Depression, mortality
    rates in US **fell** in high-unemployment areas — perhaps fewer
    cigarettes, fewer fatal traffic accidents as fewer people
    commuted

<!-- page 208: 1 nugget -->

### Creative Destruction

- [SHOULD KNOW][N113] **Creative destruction — recessions can ultimately
  improve economic efficiency**
  - **Joseph Schumpeter** (Austrian economist): bad businesses go bust;
    mediocre businesses must step up to survive; good businesses tend
    to be OK → resources freed from bad businesses flow to good or
    new ones
  - **Dot-com crash (2000)**: Nasdaq crashed after years of rising share
    prices in online companies; hype-based businesses closed; crash
    **accelerated the rise** of genuinely viable online businesses
    like **eBay**, changing consumer practices globally
  - Key insight: not all destruction is bad — crises can be the
    mechanism by which an economy reallocates resources to more
    productive uses

<!-- page 208 continued: 1 nugget -->

### How to Predict Economic Crises

- [MUST KNOW][N114] **The economist-as-meteorologist analogy — what
  prediction can and can't do**
  - Economists: good at predicting broad trends (is the economy
    heading toward boom or bust = **business cycle**); bad at
    predicting exact timing or precise trigger
  - Meteorologist analogy: knows August is warmer than December;
    can't tell you it will rain at 3:14pm Tuesday
  - Two warning signs to watch:
    1. **Disruptions in global economy**: speculative bubbles, pandemics,
       unsustainable imbalances (too much debt)
    2. **Decreases in consumer or business confidence**, which can
       spread across the whole economic system

<!-- page 209: 1 nugget -->

### Economic Models for Crisis Detection

- [SHOULD KNOW][N115] **How economists build models to spot crises**
  - Models have two components: (1) **data** and (2) **assumptions**
  - Bank of England: uses more than **100 data sets** containing
    >100 million separate bits of data
  - Key data to watch: increases in risky borrowing (led up to
    Great Depression and 2007–8) and falls in house prices
  - **Unorthodox leading indicators economists use**:
    - **Cardboard usage**: non-durable goods are shipped in cardboard;
      rising cardboard sales = companies producing more = employing more;
      when cardboard demand tapers off → economic confidence may be
      about to implode
    - **RV/camper van sales**: declines in 1989, 2000, 2006 all came
      shortly before subsequent recessions
    - **R-index** (created by *The Economist*): number of articles in
      the *New York Times* and *Washington Post* mentioning "recession";
      higher count → recession more likely; correctly predicted 1981
      and 2001 recessions; reflects the **self-fulfilling nature of
      crises** — they happen when more people start worrying and
      talking about it

<!-- page 210: 1 nugget -->

<!-- page 211: 1 nugget (R-index continued, assumptions section) -->

- [MUST KNOW][N116] **The rationality assumption — and why models fail**
  - Economic models assume people are **rational** (utility-maximising
    machines from Ch. 1); e.g. assuming people will save more during
    downturns because they fear job loss
  - In many cases this holds — until it doesn't
  - **Behavioural economics**: a booming sub-field that studies
    people's *actual* economic behaviour, which can be based on
    emotions rather than how traditional models say they should act
  - Irrationality is everywhere: it's why people continue to invest
    in obviously overpriced South Sea Company shares, or queue hours
    to buy Beanie Babies as investments
  - Models are only as good as their assumptions — when assumptions
    break down, models fail badly

<!-- page 212: 1 nugget -->

### Economists' Track Record

- [MUST KNOW][N117] **Economists' dismal record at predicting recessions**
  - **Michael Fish moment**: BBC weatherman Michael Fish famously
    laughed off a viewer's suggestion of a hurricane in 1987 —
    that night brought the most destructive storm to hit UK since
    1703 (winds up to 115 mph, hundreds of thousands of homes without
    power, 18 people killed)
  - In 2007: Bank of England predicted **1–4.5% growth** for 2008;
    while noting "turmoil in international financial markets", failed
    to predict a global economic crisis
  - **Andy Haldane** (former Bank of England Chief Economist) called
    this economists' "Michael Fish moment"
  - Damning statistic: economists have **failed to predict 148 of the
    past 153 recessions** around the world since the 1990s
  - Yet models remain the best method available: "no model is right,
    but some are useful; a flawed model is better than no model at all"

<!-- page 213: 1 nugget -->

### The Answer to the Queen's Question

- [SHOULD KNOW][N118] **Why economists missed the 2007–8 crisis —
  the British Academy letter**
  - On **22 July 2009**, a group of economists wrote to the Queen
    to answer her question about why no one saw the crisis coming
    (result of a British Academy forum on 17 June)
  - Answer: some economists *did* foresee the crisis; they just
    didn't know what exactly would trigger it, when, or how bad
    it would be — like meteorologists able to forecast rain but
    unable to say how much or when
  - Many policymakers had written about the dangers of banks
    lending mortgages to people unlikely to repay — but most
    failed to **connect the dots**: fall in house prices → problems
    in banks → wider issues across the economy
  - Models were good at predicting small/near-term risks but not
    equipped to say what those risks meant for *other parts* of
    the economy — couldn't see systemic interconnection
  - Core conclusion: every crisis is different; future crises won't
    be identical to past ones; given the complexity of economies
    and that people are not rational, it is difficult to predict
    when and what will cause crises
  - Shortest honest answer: "Predicting crises is hard, because
    the economy — and people — are complicated."
  - Implication: economists' role isn't just to *prevent* crises
    (foolhardy to think all can be prevented); it's also to
    **react to them** using the policy levers available

<!-- page 214: 1 nugget -->

---

## Chapter 10: Can't We Just Print More Money?

*On what on earth 'quantitative easing' is, why bridges to nowhere
might not always be a bad idea, and how Lord of the Rings isn't the
only New Zealand export to go global.*

<!-- page 215: no content (illustration/chapter divider) -->

### Central Banks and the Money-Printing Question

- [MUST KNOW][N119] **The scale of modern "money printing" by central
  banks**
  - Opening hook: in Spanish TV show *Money Heist*, bank robbers break
    into the Royal Mint in Madrid to print >**2 billion euros**;
    their argument (by "El Profesor") — it's not stealing, it's
    new money
  - Reality: central banks have been doing something similar,
    legally, at a far larger scale, for over a decade
  - Since the 2007–8 crisis:
    - **Bank of England**: created almost **£1 trillion** of new money
      to buy things in the economy = almost **£15,000 per person** in UK
    - **US Federal Reserve**: created over **$7 trillion**
    - **European Central Bank (ECB)**: created a similar amount of euros
  - Chapter's purpose: explain the economic levers central banks can
    use to guide the economy, what restrains them, and how
    governments also use taxation and spending to manage the economy

<!-- page 216: 1 nugget -->

### Japan's Lost Decade — The Starting Point for Monetary Policy

- [MUST KNOW][N120] **Japan 1990s: why conventional monetary policy
  hit its limits**
  - 1980s Japan = boom: global darling of capitalism, producing
    Walkmans, cars, high-tech goods
  - Early 1990s: **two bubbles burst simultaneously** — house price
    bubble and stock market bubble
  - Economy slowed → demand fell → **inflation fell** (lower demand
    = prices grow less quickly or fall; see Ch. 6)
  - Bank of Japan needed to stimulate economy and **raise inflation**
    — goal: price stability (stable prices signal healthy level of
    spending)
  - Tool chosen: **interest rates** — the standard weapon of monetary
    policy in the 1990s
  - This case study sets up why Japan later had to invent a new
    tool (QE) when interest rates hit zero

<!-- page 217: 1 nugget -->

### The Monetary Transmission Mechanism

- [MUST KNOW][N121] **How central bank interest rates flow through
  the economy — the monetary transmission mechanism**
  - Central banks are the bank for other banks — they set the terms
    on which they lend to, and pay interest on deposits from, other
    banks
  - When central banks change their rate, a series of **knock-on
    effects** ripple through the economy via multiple channels
    (collectively: the **monetary transmission mechanism**)

  **Channel 1 — Interest Rate Channel:**
  - Central bank rate change → commercial banks change what they
    charge borrowers and pay depositors
  - Direct: **tracker mortgages** move in line with central bank rate
  - Indirect: banks compete for customers → forced to pass on
    cost changes or lose business
  - Eventually feeds through to all rates: credit cards, savings
    accounts, business loans
  - Higher rates → more incentive to save, less to spend/invest →
    economy slows; lower rates → more incentive to spend today →
    economy boosts (**interest rate channel**)

<!-- page 218: 1 nugget -->

  **Channel 2 — Wealth Channel:**
  - Interest rate ↓ → prices of financial assets (stocks) and
    physical assets (houses) tend to rise
  - People holding assets become better off → they spend more of
    their increased wealth (e.g. borrowing against higher house
    value and spending it)
  - Lower interest rates → wealth increase → spending increase →
    economic boost (**wealth channel**)

  **Channel 3 — Income Effects:**
  - Works differently for savers vs. borrowers:
    - **Borrowers**: lower rates → smaller monthly mortgage payments
      → more money in pocket → spend more → boosts economy
    - **Savers**: lower rates → earn less on savings → monthly income
      falls → rein in spending → dampens economy
  - Net effect: economic evidence suggests **borrowers spend a
    larger share of new income** than savers cut spending →
    income effect from rate reductions tends to **boost** spending
    overall

<!-- page 219: 1 nugget -->

  **Channel 4 — Banking Channel:**
  - Lower rates → banks offer loans more cheaply → more demand
    for loans (people borrow to fund spending/investment today)
  - Banks more willing to lend (cost of issuing/maintaining loans
    is lower; assets offered as security are more valuable via
    wealth channel)
  - More lending → more money supply (Ch. 9) → more economic
    activity → more inflation (**banking/credit channel**)

  **Channel 5 — Exchange Rate Channel:**
  - Particularly important for small, internationally connected
    economies (e.g. UK)
  - Lower UK interest rates → sterling falls in value relative to
    currencies with higher rates (capital flows to higher-yielding
    currencies)

<!-- page 220: 1 nugget (exchange rate channel introduced, continues) -->

<!-- pp. 219-240 -->

## [Continuing chapter on Monetary Policy / Central Banking]

### Five Channels of the Monetary Transmission Mechanism

- [MUST KNOW][N100] **Channel 1: Interest rate channel**
  - Higher rates → more incentive to save, less to spend/invest
    → economy slows; lower rates → opposite → economy boosts
  - Named by economists: the **interest rate channel of the
    monetary transmission mechanism**
  - Core principle: rate changes ripple through every borrowing
    and saving decision in the economy simultaneously

<!-- page 219: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N101] **Channel 2: Wealth channel**
  - When rates fall, prices of financial and physical assets
    (stocks, houses) tend to rise
  - Higher asset prices make holders wealthier → they spend more
    ("wealth effect")
  - Example: homeowner whose house value rises borrows a bit
    more against it, then spends the cash
  - Mechanism: lower rates → higher asset prices → higher wealth
    → more spending → demand boost → upward pressure on inflation
  - Named: **wealth channel of the transmission mechanism**

- [SHOULD KNOW][N102] **Channel 3: Income effects**
  - Works differently for savers vs. borrowers:
    - Borrowers: lower rates → lower monthly mortgage payments
      → more disposable income → more spending (boost)
    - Savers: lower rates → lower interest income → less to spend
      → retrenchment (drag)
  - Most people are *both* — net effect depends on your
    personal balance of savings vs. debt
  - Across the whole economy: borrowers tend to spend more of
    their gained income than savers cut back; net effect of
    a rate cut is usually a boost to spending
  - Key caveat: if savers and borrowers had identical sensitivity,
    the effects would cancel out — they don't

<!-- page 220: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N103] **Channel 4: Bank lending channel**
  - Lower rates → banks offer cheaper loans → more demand for
    credit AND banks more willing to lend
  - Also: higher asset values (wealth channel) give borrowers
    more collateral, further loosening credit conditions
  - More lending → increases money supply → boosts activity →
    raises inflation (links back to Ch. on money creation)

- [MUST KNOW][N104] **Channel 5: Exchange rate channel**
  - Particularly important for small, open economies (UK example)
  - Mechanism: if UK rates fall but US rates hold, investors
    get better returns in dollars → money flows out of pounds
    into dollars → pound weakens → imports cost more → inflation
    rises (links to Ch. 6 on import price inflation)
  - One policy rate change triggers all five channels
    simultaneously — the combined effect shapes inflation

- [MUST KNOW][N105] **Policy lags: the moving target problem**
  - All five channels take time — monetary policy effects on
    inflation typically take **6 months to 2 years**
  - Analogy: "a sharpshooter aiming at a moving target — must
    aim ahead of where the target will be when the bullet arrives"
  - Critical implication: if today's high inflation is caused
    by something already past and not expected to last, raising
    rates now would be futile — the inflation would fade on its
    own and you'd merely slow the economy unnecessarily
  - Policymakers are constantly setting policy for where they
    *think* the economy will be, not where it actually is

<!-- page 221: 3 nuggets -->

### Japan's Lost Decade and the Birth of QE

- [MUST KNOW][N106] **Bank of Japan hits the lower bound (1991–1995)**
  - As deflation/disinflation took hold, BoJ cut rates from
    **8% to 0.5%** — the lowest in Japan's history to that point
  - Still wasn't enough: prices continued to fall
  - They had hit the **lower bound**: the point at which interest
    rates could fall no further
  - Prior to Japan, the lower bound was largely a theoretical
    concept — discussed in academic journals "almost nobody read"
  - Classical theory: rates can't go below zero because everyone
    would just hold cash, which pays 0% interest

- [SHOULD KNOW][N107] **Why the lower bound is actually slightly below zero**
  - In theory: below zero → everyone converts deposits to cash
    (which pays 0%) — you'd be "crazy to pay someone to borrow
    money when you could earn more by taking cash out of a bank"
  - In practice: cash has real costs — storage (warehouses),
    security, logistics; can't easily transfer globally
  - Pension funds need assets that move in line with their
    long-dated obligations — not cash, whose value is fixed
  - Therefore some institutions will hold bank deposits or bonds
    even at mildly negative rates; the real lower bound is
    **slightly negative**, perhaps around **-1%** in some economies
  - How far below zero depends on banking system resilience
    and the practical costs of storing cash

<!-- page 222: 2 nuggets -->

<!-- page 223: 1 nugget (continuation of lower bound + QE intro) -->

- [MUST KNOW][N108] **Quantitative easing (QE) — origin and mechanics**
  - Japan feared negative rates would damage fragile banks and
    hurt savers (Japan has very high savings rates)
  - Alternative: **quantitative easing** — print money to
    increase quantity of money in the system; also called
    **asset purchase programmes** or simply **QE**
  - Mechanics: central bank does NOT literally print banknotes
    1. Central bank buys an asset (usually a government bond)
       from a person or company in the economy
    2. Instead of paying with existing money, it simply
       credits the seller's commercial bank account at the
       central bank with new money (adds zeros)
    3. Commercial bank credits the seller's account
    4. Net result: seller has fewer bonds, more money; central
       bank has more bonds, matching deposit owed to comm. bank
    5. More money created from scratch — "without the need
       for a printer"
  - Central banks prefer low-risk assets (government bonds)
    because "we're a cautious bunch"

<!-- page 224: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N109] **QE was considered exotic and Japan-specific**
  - By mid-2000s, Bank of Japan doing QE on what felt like
    a "huge scale" — seen as unorthodox, specific to Japan
  - Some economists estimated the world would only be at the
    lower bound — needing QE — **once every 50 years**
  - Then 2008 happened: housing collapse → fragile financial
    system → central banks everywhere hit the lower bound
  - **Ben Bernanke**: academic expert on Great Depression and
    Japanese QE; became Fed Chairman 2007 — just in time
  - 2008: Fed began buying financial assets (first mortgages,
    then government bonds) with newly created money
  - Other central banks followed → **QE went global**

<!-- page 225: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N110] **Bernanke's famous joke and the QE paradox**
  > "The problem with QE is that it works well in practice,
  > but not in theory."
  > — **Ben Bernanke**
  - Not a criticism of QE — a recognition that economists know
    QE lowers long-term interest rates and boosts inflation
    empirically, but don't fully understand the mechanism
  - Analogy: general anaesthetic was used for decades before
    scientists understood *how* it worked — lack of mechanistic
    understanding didn't make it wrong to use
  - **Liquidity trap** (the theoretical problem): at zero rates,
    mainstream New Keynesian theory said people already have all
    the money they need; giving them more money-equivalents
    (bonds that also pay zero) would change nothing — they'd
    just squirrel it away; no change in behaviour
  - So economists were using a tool that their own dominant
    theory said shouldn't work

<!-- page 226: 1 nugget -->

### Two Theories of Why QE Actually Works

- [MUST KNOW][N111] **Theory 1 (New Keynesian): QE as expectations signal**
  - **New Keynesian model** (dominant through 1990s–2000s):
    QE works only insofar as it shapes *expectations* about
    future interest rates
  - Long-term rates (e.g. 30-year mortgage) are essentially the
    expected average of all future short-term rates — so
    lowering future rate expectations lowers long rates today
  - **Michael Woodford** (Columbia University): you don't
    even need QE — just a credible promise ("**forward guidance**")
    that rates will stay low for longer than expected
  - Woodford's later version: QE useful as a *commitment device*
    — central bank takes on lots of long-term debt, which means
    it would suffer big losses if it raised rates sooner than
    promised → self-binding signal that rates stay low

- [MUST KNOW][N112] **Theory 2 (Tobin): QE via portfolio rebalancing**
  - **James Tobin** (mid-20th century): people view bonds and
    money as **imperfect substitutes** — they want them for
    different reasons, not just for their interest rate
  - If you swap bonds for money (as QE does), people now have
    MORE money than they want relative to bonds → they try to
    pass on the excess money → prices get pushed up as money
    chases goods → inflation rises
  - Post-2008 epiphany: Tobin was largely right; New Keynesian
    model rested on overly strong assumptions about market
    efficiency/rationality
  - **Current consensus**: QE works through BOTH channels —
    **signalling** (forward guidance) AND
    **portfolio rebalancing** (Tobin)
  - Result: lower long-term rates → more spending and investment
    → nudges up inflation

<!-- page 227: 2 nuggets -->

<!-- page 228: continuation of QE consensus + NZ intro -->

### Central Bank Independence and Inflation Targeting

- [MUST KNOW][N113] **New Zealand 1989 — the inflation-targeting revolution**
  - **1989**: New Zealand became the first country to give its
    central bank an **explicit inflation target** set in law
    — an event that "sent aftershocks around the global economy"
    even though "the vast majority of the population may not
    have known it even happened"
  - Background: NZ had double-digit inflation through most of
    the 1970s; rates were set by politicians, not the central
    bank; politicians kept promising low inflation but hesitating
    to raise rates (unpopular, slows economy, loses votes)
  - People expected politicians to keep failing → **inflation
    bias** → self-fulfilling higher inflation
  - **Ken Rogoff** (American economist, also chess grandmaster):
    proposed the solution — take rate decisions away from
    politicians and hand to independent central bankers told to
    hit an explicit inflation target
  - Results of NZ experiment: rapid, near-immediate — inflation
    fell to target level within a few years, became far more
    stable and predictable

<!-- page 229: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N114] **Bank of England independence 1997 and the global success**
  - UK: within one week of being elected in 1997, Labour
    government granted the **Bank of England** operational
    independence — created the **Monetary Policy Committee**
    (MPC) with full control over interest rates to hit the
    inflation target
  - Bank still accountable to Parliament if it misses target,
    but politicians cannot interfere with *how* it gets there
  - **Andy Haldane** (former BoE Chief Economist): before
    independence, the best predictor of UK interest rate moves
    was "whether Mrs Thatcher had recently suffered a bad
    by-election result"
  - Global inflation track record after independence:
    - 1970s–80s: averaged >10%
    - 1990s: ~5%
    - 2000s: ~3%
    - 2010s: ~2%
  - The independence + inflation targeting model was so
    successful it was emulated by "almost every advanced
    economy in the world" within 20 years

<!-- page 230: 1 nugget -->

### The Limits of Money Printing

- [SHOULD KNOW][N115] **Why QE cannot run forever**
  - Central banks can print money up to a point — QE is
    beneficial when inflation is below target
  - Once QE pushes inflation above target, further printing
    erodes purchasing power and raises costs for business
    (as per Ch. 6 on inflation)
  - Central banks have the power to set interest rates AND the
    printing press, but independence means they are **mandated
    to protect the value of money** — they cannot print
    without limit
  - The answer to "can't we just print more money?" is: yes,
    but only to a point, within the inflation target constraint

### Fiscal Policy: The Government's Other Lever

- [MUST KNOW][N116] **Japan's *hakomono* (white elephant) fiscal stimulus**
  - **Hakomono** (Japanese: "white elephant") — term that became
    common in Japan 1990s–2000s for wasteful infrastructure
  - 1991–2008: Japanese government spent **$6.3 trillion** on
    construction-related public investments — dams, roads, bridges
  - Famous example: **$250 million bridge** from Okinawa (large
    island) to Kourijima (population: fewer than 400 people)
  - Another: art gallery in Nagoya that cost so much there was
    "nothing left over to buy art to fill it"
  - **Keynes** had argued it's better for government to pay
    people to bury money in a hole and dig it up again than to
    leave them unemployed — even absurd spending creates
    economic activity

<!-- page 231: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N117] **Why governments spend: four rationales**
  1. Subsidise under-produced goods with positive externalities
     (e.g. further education)
  2. Build large infrastructure with economies of scale that
     wouldn't otherwise get built (roads, undersea tunnels)
  3. Redistribute income to address social inequality
  4. **Fiscal stimulus**: boost demand when economy is sluggish
     — the "G" in the GDP equation (Ch. 4)
  - Government spending is economically equivalent to any other
    spending — buys real goods and services, raises output
  - Traditional view: fiscal policy is "**too slow and too
    political**" — infrastructure takes years to design and
    deploy; politicians are incentivised to spend now, defer pain

### The Fiscal Multiplier Debate

- [MUST KNOW][N118] **The fiscal multiplier — Keynes's big idea**
  - **Keynes**: government spending effect can be *greater* than
    the amount spent, especially when the economy is sluggish
  - Mechanism (the multiplier chain):
    1. Government pays workers to dig holes
    2. Workers now have more money; spend some on bread
    3. Baker earns more, spends some of that
    4. And so on — each round adds economic activity
  - Each pound of government spending may generate **£1.50** (or
    more) of economic output — multiplier > 1
  - Became theory of choice in 1930s–40s; influenced
    **President Franklin D. Roosevelt's New Deal** (massive
    state expenditure to fight Great Depression)

<!-- page 232: 1 nugget -->

- [MUST KNOW][N119] **Three reasons fiscal multiplier may be < 1 (critics)**
  1. **Monetary policy crowding out**: government spending →
     inflation risk → central bank raises rates → private
     spending/investment falls → partially offsets fiscal boost
  2. **Tax financing**: government must eventually fund spending
     through taxes → taxpayers have less to spend → their
     reduced spending may match or exceed new government spending
  3. **Ricardian Equivalence** (named after **David Ricardo**):
     rational people, anticipating future tax rises to pay for
     current borrowing, rein in spending *today* to prepare
     → completely offsets the stimulus
  - Most economists think Ricardian Equivalence doesn't fully
    hold in practice because:
    - Tax payers ≠ spending recipients (redistribution occurs)
    - People don't actually think 20 years ahead when making
      spending decisions
    - Human behaviour is often not perfectly rational

<!-- page 233: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N120] **When is the fiscal multiplier high vs. low?**
  - Economists' estimates of the multiplier range from
    **less than 1 to well over 2** — one of the most
    contested numbers in economics
  - The multiplier is higher when:
    - Interest rates are very low (monetary policymakers won't
      raise rates to offset the fiscal boost — they welcome it)
    - Economy is in a **liquidity trap** (government picks up
      money people refuse to spend; no crowding out)
    - Spending goes on productive infrastructure that
      boosts long-run growth (not day-to-day frills or
      "people digging holes")
  - Japan's 1990s bridges-to-nowhere may have been less silly
    than they looked — a high-multiplier environment

- [SHOULD KNOW][N121] **Taxation as the mirror of spending**
  - Taxes are the other fiscal lever: they reduce disposable
    income → cut demand → slow economy
  - Cutting taxes puts money into private hands → boosts demand
    → can have similar multiplier effect to spending increases
  - **Progressive taxes**: fall disproportionately on rich
  - **Regressive taxes**: fall disproportionately on poor
  - Tax debate mirrors spending debate — the size of the tax
    multiplier is equally contested

<!-- page 234: 2 nuggets -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N122] **Automatic stabilisers — fiscal policy on autopilot**
  - Governments don't always need active policy decisions —
    budget design creates **automatic stabilisers**:
    - Economy slows → unemployment rises → welfare spending
      automatically increases → tax receipts automatically fall
      → net fiscal boost delivered without any new legislation
    - Economy booms → welfare spending falls automatically →
      tax receipts rise automatically → natural fiscal brake
  - Operates entirely without ministerial action or legislation
  - Complements but doesn't replace deliberate fiscal policy

### Government Borrowing and Debt Sustainability

- [MUST KNOW][N123] **How governments borrow and why it matters**
  - When spending > taxes, government issues **bonds** (IOUs)
    — bought mainly by institutions with long-duration liabilities
    (pension funds, insurance companies)
  - Bonds are usually lowest-cost borrowing in the economy
    because governments are considered safest borrowers
  - **Argentina default, 26 December 2001**: defaulted on
    **$95 billion** of obligations after 3+ years of recession
    — offered creditors just 30 cents on the dollar
  - Consequence: Argentina's borrowing rate is now ~**50%**
    vs. near-zero for other developed governments — making
    fiscal policy nearly impossible to fund
  - Argentina has defaulted multiple times; history of
    unreliability creates a permanent risk premium that
    compounds economic disadvantage

<!-- page 235: 1 nugget -->

<!-- page 236: continuation of debt discussion -->

- [MUST KNOW][N124] **Why the "government debt = household debt" analogy fails**
  - Common argument: "like a good household, government must
    tighten belt and pay back debt eventually"
  - Why it's a poor analogy:
    1. **Governments don't die** — can roll over debt across
       generations; individuals are constrained to ~80 years
    2. **Investment that grows the tax base**: if £1bn motorway
       generates £2bn of economic activity, the economy is bigger
       when the debt comes due → debt/GDP ratio *falls* even
       though nominal debt *rose*
    3. **Growth makes debt manageable**: if economy grows at 2%
       and interest rate is 1%, you can borrow up to 1% of GDP
       annually and the debt-to-GDP ratio still falls naturally
    4. **Austerity trap**: if you cut so aggressively that growth
       slows to zero, the debt burden becomes *worse*, not better
       — the very act of repaying can become self-defeating
  - Key metric: **debt-to-GDP ratio** (not absolute debt level)

<!-- page 239: 1 nugget -->

- [SHOULD KNOW][N125] **Greece and the austerity trap (early 2010s)**
  - Many Eurozone countries post-2008 crisis were struggling
    to pay interest on debt (much of it from bailing out banks)
  - Problem was most severe in **Greece**
  - When Greece tried to cut spending and raise taxes to repay
    debt, it slowed growth → tax revenues fell → debt-to-GDP
    ratio worsened despite austerity
  - Classic illustration of the self-defeating dynamic: trying
    to sort out finances while in recession can make the debt
    situation worse (introduced here, likely developed further
    in subsequent pages)

<!-- page 240: 1 nugget -->

<!-- pp. 239-260 -->

## Chapter 10 (continued): Can Governments Just Keep On Spending Forever?

### Government Debt and Default Risk

<!-- page 239: 3 nuggets -->

- **Long-duration lenders** — pension funds and insurance companies — park money
  for decades and need reliable repayment; government bonds serve this role
  [SHOULD KNOW][N126]
- **Argentina default, 26 December 2001** — after 3+ years of recession the
  government defaulted on over **$95 billion** in obligations; creditors
  received just **30 cents on the dollar** [MUST KNOW][N127]
- Argentina's **serial default history** means bond markets demand near-**50%
  interest rates** on Argentine debt vs near-zero in stable economies — a
  permanent borrowing penalty that makes productive investment much harder
  [MUST KNOW][N128]

<!-- page 240: 3 nuggets -->

- Most governments are reliable debtors and therefore enjoy **some of the lowest
  borrowing costs in the economy** [SHOULD KNOW][N129]
- How much government debt is acceptable is a **hotly contested question** —
  economists remain divided on whether running a **deficit** (taxes < spending)
  indefinitely is acceptable [MUST KNOW][N130]
- **Deficit** = gap when taxes < spending; **surplus** = opposite; the debate
  centres on whether deficits are inherently problematic or manageable tools
  [SHOULD KNOW][N131]

### The Household Analogy — Why It Fails

<!-- page 241: 4 nuggets -->

- The "tighten your belt like a household" analogy is **flawed for two reasons**:
  (1) governments don't die — they can roll debt across much longer horizons
  than any individual lifespan; (2) productive debt that grows the economy can
  shrink the **debt-to-GDP ratio** even while nominal debt rises [MUST KNOW][N132]

> "Even though the amount of debt has gone up, the amount of debt relative to
> the size of the economy has gone down."
> — Patel & Meaning

- Motorway example: borrowing £1bn to build infrastructure that generates £2bn
  long-run GDP growth means the **debt pays for itself** — bigger economy, more
  tax base, ratio falls [MUST KNOW][N133]
- Key sustainability rule: **if the new debt taken on is less than the rate at
  which the economy grows**, debt-to-GDP falls naturally over time; e.g. 2%
  growth + 1% interest = safe to borrow up to 1% of GDP per year without ratio
  rising [MUST KNOW][N134]

<!-- page 242: 3 nuggets -->

- **Self-defeating austerity trap**: if paying down debt requires so much taxation
  or spending cuts that growth slows to zero, the very act of fiscal tightening
  makes the debt problem *worse* not better [MUST KNOW][N135]
- **Greece and the Eurozone crisis (turn of the 2010s)**: governments struggling
  to service debt interest (partly from bailing out banks post-2008) implemented
  austerity — cut spending, raised taxes — which slowed growth and reduced the
  tax base available to repay debt [MUST KNOW][N136]
- **IMF fiscal multiplier miscalculation**: then-Managing Director **Christine
  Lagarde** later conceded the IMF had assumed a fiscal multiplier of less than
  one when it was in fact closer to **1.7** — meaning spending cuts shrank the
  economy far more than projected; IMF had pressured Greece to cut ill-advisedly
  [MUST KNOW][N137]

<!-- page 243: 3 nuggets -->

- Symmetric logic: just as fiscal boosts have outsized positive effects when
  multiplier > 1, **austerity policies have outsized negative effects** in the
  same situation [MUST KNOW][N138]
- Even **Keynesian economists** accept that if borrowing is so high that markets
  question repayment ability, borrowing costs rise — hurting both current and
  future taxpayers [SHOULD KNOW][N139]
- **100% debt-to-GDP is not a cliff edge**: evidence from UK shows that for the
  last 100 years, UK government debt has been at or above 100% of GDP for
  *almost half that time* — without serious repayment concerns [MUST KNOW][N140]

### Money Heist (La Casa de Papel) — El Profesor's Argument

<!-- page 243-244: 2 nuggets -->

- **El Profesor** (fictional) argues that breaking into the Spanish Royal Mint to
  print money is equivalent to what the **European Central Bank** did — making
  "171 billion euros out of nowhere" — and calls it a "liquidity injection for
  the real economy, not the banks" [NICE TO KNOW][N141]

> "'Liquidity injections', they called it. I'm making a liquidity injection but
> not for the banks. I'm doing it here, in the real economy."
> — El Profesor, *Money Heist*

<!-- page 244: 3 nuggets -->

- Authors' verdict: El Profesor is **partly right** — money printing can work
  through multiple channels to support activity and raise inflation when it would
  otherwise be too low [SHOULD KNOW][N142]
- **The limit on printing**: unlimited printing produces too much inflation,
  eroding the value of newly printed money faster than it helps people —
  potentially making them worse off [MUST KNOW][N143]
- Unlike the bank robbers, the **Bank of England's money-printing** (QE) operates
  under a democratically set **inflation target**, ensuring it cannot spiral out
  of control — the institutional constraint is what separates responsible QE from
  reckless printing [MUST KNOW][N144]

