- Pages 279-282 contain source endnotes for Chapters 9, 10,
  and the Conclusion. No new conceptual content — these are
  citation references only.
- Notable footnote of interest (Ch. 10, note 2): **Silvio Gesell**,
  a German economist, proposed charging interest on paper money
  via a stamp fee on banknotes (they would expire if not stamped),
  known as a **Gesell tax**. Discussed in various forms by
  **Irving Fisher** and **John Maynard Keynes**.
- Ch. 10, note 4: Japan's early QE largely bought assets from banks,
  but many banks were impaired post-crash and hoarded the money.
  **Ben Bernanke**'s revamped QE bypassed banks and bought
  directly from households/companies, getting money more directly
  into the economy.
- Ch. 10, note 7: The UK's original inflation target was 2.5%
  on **RPI-X**; later updated to 2% **CPI**.
- Conclusion notes reference surveys showing public trust in
  economic experts is low (YouGov, ING-Economics Network,
  ESCOE surveys, 2017-2020).

